Yes — in Mathematica you can combine a 3D histogram with 2D histograms on the sides by using `Histogram3D` for the joint distribution and then mapping the 1D histograms as textures onto polygons in 3D space.

A compact version of the approach is:

```wl
data = RandomVariate[
   MultinormalDistribution[{0, 0}, {{1, 0.9}, {0.9, 2}}],
   10^4
];

lightSources = {
   {"Directional", White, Scaled[{1, 0, 1}]},
   {"Directional", White, Scaled[{1, .5, 1}]},
   {"Directional", White, Scaled[{.5, 0, 1}]}
};

G1 = Histogram3D[
   data, {0.25}, "PDF",
   ColorFunction -> "Rainbow",
   PlotRange -> {{-4, 4}, {-4, 4}, All},
   ImageSize -> Medium,
   Boxed -> False,
   Lighting -> lightSources
];

G3 = Histogram[
   Transpose[data][[1]], {-4, 4, .25}, "Probability",
   ColorFunction -> Function[{h}, ColorData["Rainbow"][h]],
   Axes -> False
];

G4 = Histogram[
   Transpose[data][[2]], {-4, 4, .25}, "Probability",
   ColorFunction -> Function[{h}, ColorData["Rainbow"][h]],
   Axes -> False
];

G5 = Graphics3D[{
   EdgeForm[],
   Texture[G3],
   Polygon[
     {{4, 4, 0}, {-4, 4, 0}, {-4, 4, 0.2}, {4, 4, 0.2}},
     VertexTextureCoordinates -> {{0, 0}, {1, 0}, {1, 1}, {0, 1}}
   ]
   },
   Axes -> False, Boxed -> False
];

G6 = Graphics3D[{
   EdgeForm[],
   Texture[G4],
   Polygon[
     {{-4, 4, 0}, {-4, -4, 0}, {-4, -4, 0.2}, {-4, 4, 0.2}},
     VertexTextureCoordinates -> {{0, 0}, {1, 0}, {1, 1}, {0, 1}}
   ]
   },
   Axes -> False, Boxed -> False
];

Show[G1, G5, G6]
```

What this does:
- `G1` draws the central 3D histogram.
- `G3` and `G4` compute the marginal histograms for each coordinate.
- `G5` and `G6` place those marginal histograms as textured “walls” beside the 3D plot.

