Yes — this is a known issue in that version/setting, and the fix is to change the DAE constraint handling.

Use:

Method -> {"IndexReduction" -> {Automatic, "ConstraintMethod" -> "Projection"}}

instead of just:

Method -> {"IndexReduction" -> Automatic}

Why this helps:
- Your system is a DAE with algebraic constraints.
- With the default index-reduction handling, the numeric solution may drift slightly and appear to violate the initial constraints.
- `"ConstraintMethod" -> "Projection"` forces the solution back onto the constraint manifold, so the initial conditions and constraints are respected much more reliably.

Also, because your coefficients are written as machine-precision numbers, it helps to rationalize them first. Mixed approximate arithmetic can contribute to numerical inconsistency.

Suggested version:

solution = NDSolve[
  Rationalize@{
    (* equations and initial conditions *)
  },
  {a[t], b[t], c[t], l[t]},
  {t, 0., 0.25},
  Method -> {"IndexReduction" -> {Automatic, "ConstraintMethod" -> "Projection"}}
];

Then check:

asol[t_] = a[t] /. Flatten[solution];
Print["a[0] = ", asol[0], ", a'[0] = ", Derivative[1][asol][0]]

Expected result:
- `a[0]` matches the initial value
- `a'[0]` is essentially zero, up to numerical roundoff

So this is not really a bug in `NDSolve` so much as a limitation of the default DAE handling in that Mathematica version, and the projection constraint method is the right workaround.

Yes. The front end does not preserve indentation in package editing by default, but you can make it do so by changing the notebook’s code style settings.

The key style options are:

- AutoIndent -> True
- AutoSpacing -> True
- LineBreakWithin -> Automatic
- LineIndent -> 1
- LinebreakAdjustments -> {0.85, 2, 10, 0, 1}

A practical way to apply this is to modify the package notebook’s style definitions so that the "Code" style uses these settings. The accepted workaround is essentially:

```wl
modifyCodeStyle[nb_NotebookObject, Notebook] :=
 Module[{styledefs, editable},
  editable = Editable /. Options[nb, Editable];
  SetOptions[nb, Editable -> True];
  styledefs = StyleDefinitions /. Options[nb, StyleDefinitions];
  Switch[Head[styledefs],
   Notebook,
   styledefs = Notebook[
     {
      First@First[styledefs],
      Cell[
       StyleData["Code"],
       PageWidth -> WindowWidth,
       AutoIndent -> True,
       AutoSpacing -> True,
       LineBreakWithin -> Automatic,
       LineIndent -> 1,
       LinebreakAdjustments -> {0.85, 2, 10, 0, 1}
      ],
      Rest@First[styledefs]
     },
     Sequence @@ Rest[List @@ styledefs]
   ],
   String | FileName | FrontEnd`FileName,
   styledefs = Notebook[
     {
      Cell[StyleData[StyleDefinitions -> styledefs]],
      Cell[
       StyleData["Code"],
       PageWidth -> WindowWidth,
       AutoIndent -> True,
       AutoSpacing -> True,
       LineBreakWithin -> Automatic,
       LineIndent -> 1,
       LinebreakAdjustments -> {0.85, 2, 10, 0, 1}
      ]
     },
     StyleDefinitions -> "PrivateStylesheetFormatting.nb"
   ]
  ];
  SetOptions[nb, StyleDefinitions -> styledefs];
  SetOptions[nb, Editable -> editable]
 ]
```

Then call it on the selected notebook:

```wl
modifyCodeStyle[SelectedNotebook[], Notebook]
```

In short: yes, it’s possible, but you generally do it by editing the notebook’s stylesheet/style definitions rather than via a simple front-end preference. If you want, I can also show a shorter version tailored specifically for package notebooks.

If I understand correctly, you want to build all elements of

B = { {x, {x, y}} | x in A, y in A }

with

A = { ∅, {∅}, {{∅}, ∅} }.

The idea is to take every ordered pair (x, y) from A × A, and for each pair form the set

{x, {x, y}}.

Since A has 3 elements, there are 3 × 3 = 9 such pairs, so B has 9 elements.

Writing them out explicitly, using ES for ∅:

1. {ES, {ES, ES}}
2. {ES, {ES, {ES}}}
3. {ES, {ES, {{ES}, ES}}}
4. {{ES}, {{ES}, ES}}
5. {{ES}, {{ES}, {ES}}}
6. {{ES}, {{ES}, {{ES}, ES}}}
7. {{{ES}, ES}, {{{ES}, ES}, ES}}
8. {{{ES}, ES}, {{{ES}, ES}, {ES}}}
9. {{{ES}, ES}, {{{ES}, ES}, {{ES}, ES}}}

So B is exactly the set containing those 9 sets.

If you want to generate this in Mathematica, one way is:

a = {∅, {∅}, {{∅}, ∅}};
b = {#, {#, #2}} & @@@ Tuples[a, {2}];

Or equivalently:

b = Table[{x, {x, y}}, {x, a}, {y, a}] // Flatten[#, 1] &;

Both give the same result.

If your goal is to get Fourier coefficients from the numeric solution returned by `NDSolve`, you should not try to feed the `InterpolatingFunction` directly into `FourierSinSeries`. That function expects an explicit symbolic function of one variable, not a multidimensional `InterpolatingFunction` object.

What you can do instead is:

1. evaluate the interpolating function at numeric points,
2. integrate against the Fourier basis functions numerically.

For example, for a fixed time `t0`, define the coefficients as

```mathematica
an[n_] := NIntegrate[
  uSol[t0, x] Cos[n Pi x/xMax],
  {x, xMin, xMax}
];

bn[n_] := NIntegrate[
  uSol[t0, x] Sin[n Pi x/xMax],
  {x, xMin, xMax}
];
```

Then call them with a numeric `n`, e.g.

```mathematica
an[1]
bn[1]
```

Important point: `n` must be numeric inside `NIntegrate`. If you write `an = NIntegrate[..., Cos[n ...]]` with `n` left symbolic, `NIntegrate` will complain because it cannot sample the integrand numerically.

Example pattern:

```mathematica
With[{n = 1, t0 = 0.1 TMax},
  an = NIntegrate[uSol[t0, x] Cos[n Pi x/xMax], {x, xMin, xMax}];
  bn = NIntegrate[uSol[t0, x] Sin[n Pi x/xMax], {x, xMin, xMax}];
]
```

If you want the full Fourier series approximation up to order `N`, you can build it from these coefficients.

If you want, I can also show how to write a small function that computes the truncated Fourier approximation from the `InterpolatingFunction` automatically.

You can name multiple input fields by giving each control its own symbol, then conditionally include the extra controls with `If` and `Sequence @@`.

Example:

```wl
Manipulate[
  {a, b, c},
  Dynamic@Column@{
    Control[{a, 0}],
    Control[{ShowMore, {False, True}}],
    Sequence @@ If[ShowMore,
      {
        Control[{b, 0}],
        Control[{c, 0}]
      },
      {}
    ]
  }
]
```

How it works:
- `a`, `b`, `c` are the variables tied to the input fields.
- `ShowMore` controls whether the extra fields appear.
- `Sequence @@ If[...]` inserts zero, one, or more controls into the list dynamically.

