The usual trick is to close the cut-out wedge with polygon faces, and then apply the same styling to those faces as to the curved surface. That way the object looks solid instead of hollow.

A simple pattern is:

1. Use `SphericalPlot3D` or `ParametricPlot3D` for the curved part.
2. Capture the sampled boundary points along the cut.
3. Build `Polygon`s from those boundary points.
4. Add those polygons with the same `PlotStyle`.

Example:

```mathematica
sph[r_, theta_, phi_] := 
  r {Sin[theta] Cos[phi], Sin[theta] Sin[phi], Cos[theta]};

wedge = With[{r = 1 + Sin[5 phi]/5},
  Module[{plot, pts},
    {plot, {pts}} = Reap @ SphericalPlot3D[
       r, {theta, 0, Pi}, {phi, 0.5, 1.8},
       Mesh -> None,
       PlotStyle -> Yellow,
       EvaluationMonitor :> Sow[{theta, phi}]
    ];

    Show[
      plot,
      Graphics3D[{
        Yellow,
        Polygon[
          Apply[
            Function[{theta, phi}, sph[r, theta, phi]],
            {
              Reverse @ Sort @ Cases[pts, {_, Min[Last /@ pts]}],
              Sort @ Cases[pts, {_, Max[Last /@ pts]}]
            },
            {2}
          ]
        ]
      }]
    ]
  ]
];
```

This closes the wedge with two flat faces.

If you want an isometric-style view, you can set the view matrix manually, or just use `ViewPoint`/`Boxed -> False`. An isometric projection can be approximated by choosing a viewpoint along a diagonal direction such as `{1, 1, 1}`.

For a fully custom cut or more advanced shading, `ParametricPlot3D` plus manually constructed boundary polygons often gives more control.

If you want, I can also provide:
- a cleaner version for a true ellipsoid,
- a version with color mapped by a function on the interior face,
- or an explicit isometric-view setup.

To add coordinate axes to the 3D plot, you can use `Graphics3D` with `Axes -> True` and `AxesLabel -> {"x","y","z"}`.

For your case, the main issue is that `Image3D` and `Graphics3D` use different coordinate conventions, so the spheres and axes won’t line up unless you transform the image data appropriately.

A working version is:

```wl
z = 10;
p = RandomInteger[100, {z, 3}];
r = RandomInteger[10, {z}];

spheres =
  MapThread[
    Transpose[#, {3, 2, 1}] & @ Reverse @
      RotateLeft[DiskMatrix[#1, {100, 100, 100}], #2 - {50, 50, 50}] &,
    {r, p}
  ];

i = Image3D[Plus @@ spheres, Axes -> True, AxesLabel -> {"x", "y", "z"}];

obj = GraphicsComplex[p, Sphere[Range[z], r]];
gr = Graphics3D[
  obj,
  Axes -> True,
  AxesLabel -> {"x", "y", "z"},
  PlotRange -> {{0, 100}, {0, 100}, {0, 100}}
];

Show[i, gr, Axes -> True]
```

Key points:
- `DiskMatrix` centers the object at `{50,50,50}`, so you need to shift by `#2 - {50,50,50}`.
- `Image3D` and `Graphics3D` have different axis orientations, so `Reverse` and `Transpose[#, {3,2,1}] &` are needed to align them.

If you want, I can also show a simpler version that only adds axes to a `Graphics3D` sphere plot without combining it with `Image3D`.

