`Plot` can take multiple functions, but `RegionFunction` applies to the whole `Plot`, not separately to each curve. If you want different conditions on different functions, make separate plots and combine them with `Show`, or use `Piecewise` if the result is really one function.

For your example, `Piecewise` is the cleanest solution:

```wl
pw = Piecewise[{
   {2 Sqrt[x], 0 <= x <= 1},
   {4 - 2 x,   1 < x < 2.5},
   {2 x - 7,   2.5 <= x <= 4}
   }, Indeterminate];

Plot[pw, {x, 0, 4}]
```

If you want to keep the pieces separate, do this instead:

```wl
g1 = Plot[2 Sqrt[x], {x, 0, 1}, PlotStyle -> Red];
g2 = Plot[4 - 2 x, {x, 1, 2.5}, PlotStyle -> Blue];
g3 = Plot[2 x - 7, {x, 2.5, 4}, PlotStyle -> Green];

Show[g1, g2, g3]
```

If you have many functions, you can automate it:

```wl
funcs = {2 Sqrt[x], 4 - 2 x, 2 x - 7};
domains = {{x, 0, 1}, {x, 1, 2.5}, {x, 2.5, 4}};
styles = {Red, Blue, Green};

MapThread[
  Plot[#1, #2, PlotStyle -> #3] &,
  {funcs, domains, styles}
] // Show
```

So the main answer is:
- use `Piecewise` for one conditional function,
- or make multiple `Plot`s and combine them with `Show` for separate regions/functions.

If you want to add text labels to a `ListLogLinearPlot`, the main thing is:

- don’t try to make the plot itself “dynamic” in the way a `LocatorPane` expects
- instead, put the plot in the background
- keep a separate list of label positions
- draw the labels in an `Epilog`/`Graphics` layer
- wrap only the parts that need to update in `Dynamic`

A working pattern is like this:

```wl
SeedRandom[1];
data = Accumulate[RandomReal[{-0.2, 0.2}, 300]];
thirds = Partition[data, 100];
interps = Interpolation /@ thirds;
myplot = ListLogLinearPlot[thirds];

DynamicModule[{pos = MapThread[{Log@#, #2[#]} &, {{5, 16, 50}, interps}]},
 LocatorPane[
  Dynamic[pos,
   (pos = MapThread[
      Function[{pt, interp},
       {#, interp@Exp@#} & @ Clip[First@pt, Log@interp["Domain"][[1]]]
       ],
      {#, interps}
      ]) &
   ],
  Show[
   myplot,
   Graphics[
    {
     Dynamic@Table[
       Inset[
        Framed[
         Text@TraditionalForm[i],
         RoundingRadius -> 5,
         Background -> White
         ],
        pos[[i]]
        ],
       {i, 3}
       ]
     }
    ]
   ],
  Appearance -> None
  ]
 ]
```

What this does:

- `myplot` is the base plot
- `pos` stores label locations
- `LocatorPane` lets you drag the label positions
- `Show[myplot, Graphics[…]]` overlays the labels on the plot
- `Dynamic@Table[...]` makes the label positions update live

A few important notes:

1. `pos` must be a list of coordinates, not the plot itself.
2. The plot goes in the second argument of `LocatorPane`.
3. The first argument of `LocatorPane` should be the positions of the labels/locators.
4. Anything that changes should be inside `Dynamic`.

If you’re not actually trying to drag the labels, and just want static text labels on the plot, then you can simplify a lot and use only `Epilog`:

```wl
ListLogLinearPlot[
 thirds,
 Epilog -> {
   Inset[Style["label 1", White, Background -> Black], {5, 0.1}],
   Inset[Style["label 2", White, Background -> Black], {16, 0.2}],
   Inset[Style["label 3", White, Background -> Black], {50, 0.3}]
   }
 ]
```

If you want, I can also show you how to adapt this directly to your `ruleData` / `names` / `tt[...]` setup.

