A good way to reduce memory is to separate the expensive scalar computation from the bookkeeping.

Instead of having `cfn` return `{list1[[i]], list2[[j]], scalar}` for every pair, have it return only the scalar first. Then filter on the scalar values, and only for the survivors attach the original vectors.

Here is the pattern:

```wl
cfn[l1_, l2_] := (#.#) &[l1 - l2 + 1]

list1 = Developer`ToPackedArray[list1];
list2 = Developer`ToPackedArray[list2];

data = Outer[cfn, list1, list2, 1];

p = Position[data, x_ /; cmin < x < cmax, {2}, Heads -> False];

selectdata = Transpose[{
   list1[[p[[All, 1]]]],
   list2[[p[[All, 2]]]],
   Extract[data, p]
}];
```

What this does:
- `Outer[cfn, list1, list2, 1]` computes only the scalar results.
- `Position[...]` finds the entries in the desired range.
- `Extract[data, p]` pulls out the matching scalar values.
- The original vectors are attached only for matching pairs.

This is usually much lighter on memory than storing `{vector1, vector2, scalar}` for every pair.

If memory is still an issue, the next step would be to avoid forming the full `Outer` table at all and instead scan in blocks or with `ParallelTable`/`Reap`/`Sow` style accumulation. But for many cases, this two-stage approach is already a big improvement.

