Mathematica doesn’t have a built-in “text on a path” tool like Illustrator, but you can approximate it by placing each character along a curve and rotating it to match the local tangent.

A simple approach is:

1. Split the string into characters
2. Compute equally spaced positions along the x-axis
3. Map each x-position to a y-value on the curve
4. Rotate each character by the slope of the curve at that point
5. Use the character’s baseline/descender as the anchor

Here is a basic example for a “rainbow” style along a sine curve:

```wl
textOnCurve[str_, curve_, {x0_, x1_}] := Module[
  {chars, n, xs, pts, angs, dy, dx},

  chars = Characters[str];
  n = Length[chars];
  xs = Subdivide[x0, x1, n - 1];

  pts = Table[{x, curve[x]}, {x, xs}];
  angs = Table[
    ArcTan[1, D[curve[t], t] /. t -> x],
    {x, xs}
  ];

  Graphics[
    Table[
      {
        GeometricTransformation[
          Text[Style[chars[[i]], 20], pts[[i]], {0, -1}],
          RotationTransform[angs[[i]], pts[[i]]]
        ]
      },
      {i, n}
    ],
    PlotRange -> All
  ]
]
```

Example usage:

```wl
textOnCurve[
  "The quick brown fox jumped over the lazy dog",
  2 Sin[#/3] &,
  {0, 40}
]
```

A few notes:

- `Text[..., pt, {0, -1}]` places the text using the bottom of the character as the anchor, which is close to your “use the descender” requirement.
- `ArcTan[1, slope]` gives the tangent angle of the curve.
- This is a character-by-character placement, so it’s not true font outlining or kerning-aware shaping, but it works reasonably well for display.

If you want better spacing, you can refine the x-positions using actual character widths (via `StringDimensions` or `FontMeasurements`) instead of equal spacing.

