- a version that follows any parametric curve,
- or a version that keeps the text upright rather than tangent to the curve.

Yes — you can make this much faster than nested `Table` + `If`.

The main idea is to avoid checking every threshold separately for every element. Instead, sort by `newMat`, accumulate weights once, and then expand the step values.

A faster pattern is:

```wl
process2[x_, w_, {y0_, dy_, ny_}] :=
 Module[{x0, w0},
  x0 = Ceiling[(x - y0)/dy] ~Clip~ {0, ny};
  x0 = Join[{0}, Sort@x0, {ny}];
  w0 = {0} ~Join~ Accumulate[w[[Ordering@x]]];
  Join @@ MapThread[ConstantArray, {w0, Differences@x0}]
 ]
```

Then use it like this:

```wl
bNum = 200;
nNum = 100;

weights = RandomReal[{-10, 10}, {bNum, nNum}];
newMat  = RandomReal[{-10, 10}, {bNum, nNum}];

sortMat = Range[-10, 10, 0.1];

result = MapThread[process2[#1, #2, {-10, 0.1, 201}] &, {newMat, weights}];
```

Why this is faster:
- `If` inside large nested `Table` is expensive.
- Repeated summation over all `nNum` for each threshold is expensive.
- Sorting once and using `Accumulate` reduces the work dramatically.

Also, your random data generation can be simplified and sped up:

```wl
weights = RandomReal[{-10, 10}, {bNum, nNum}];
newMat  = RandomReal[{-10, 10}, {bNum, nNum}];
```

instead of using `Table[RandomReal[...] , ...]`.

