You can replace the nested `For`/`AppendTo` structure with nested `Table`, while still computing the `j`-dependent values only once per `j`.

A direct translation is:

```mathematica
Table[
  Table[
    p = test[[i, j, 1]];
    q = test[[i, j, 2]];
    r = Cross[p, q];
    mat = Orthogonalize[{p, q, r}];
    Table[
      mat.SeparationVector[p, test[[i, k, 1]], 2],
      {k, 5}
    ],
    {j, 5}
  ],
  {i, 10}
]
```

This preserves the intended reuse of `p`, `q`, `r`, and `mat` for each `j`, instead of recomputing them for every `k`.

If you want it a bit cleaner, you can localize the temporary variables with `Module`:

```mathematica
Table[
  Table[
    Module[{p, q, r, mat},
      p = test[[i, j, 1]];
      q = test[[i, j, 2]];
      r = Cross[p, q];
      mat = Orthogonalize[{p, q, r}];
      Table[
        mat.SeparationVector[p, test[[i, k, 1]], 2],
        {k, 5}
      ]
    ],
    {j, 5}
  ],
  {i, 10}
]
```

Notes:
- `Table` is generally preferable to `AppendTo` for performance.
- If you want a flattened list like your original `res`, you can add `Flatten[..., 2]` at the end.

For example:

```mathematica
Flatten[
  Table[
    Table[
      Module[{p, q, r, mat},
        p = test[[i, j, 1]];
        q = test[[i, j, 2]];
        r = Cross[p, q];
        mat = Orthogonalize[{p, q, r}];
        Table[
          mat.SeparationVector[p, test[[i, k, 1]], 2],
          {k, 5}
        ]
      ],
      {j, 5}
    ],
    {i, 10}
  ],
  2
]
```

