To make a tangent line that moves along the curve, interpolate your data, take the derivative of the interpolating function, then draw a line through the chosen point with that slope.

Example:

```wl
data = {{5 10^-3, 0}, {4.65 10^-3, 100}, {4.32 10^-3, 200},
        {3.74 10^-3, 400}, {3.23 10^-3, 600}, {2.6 10^-3, 900},
        {2.09 10^-3, 1200}, {1.68 10^-3, 1500}};

dint = Interpolation[data];
slope = dint';

With[{x = .003, h = 800},
  y = dint[x];
  dir = Normalize[{1, slope[x]}]/2;
  ListLinePlot[data,
    Epilog -> Line[{{x, y} - h dir, {x, y} + h dir}]]
]
```

How it works:
- `Interpolation[data]` creates a smooth curve through the points.
- `dint'` gives the derivative function, i.e. the slope at each `x`.
- `x = .003` is the point where you want the tangent.
- The `Line[...]` is drawn using the slope at that point.

If you want tangents at several positions, you can do:

```wl
With[{pos = {.002, .003, .0045}, h = 400},
 ListLinePlot[data, Axes -> None,
  Epilog -> Table[
    With[{y = dint[x], dir = Normalize[{1, slope[x]}]/2},
      {AbsoluteThickness[2],
       Line[{{x, y} - h dir, {x, y} + h dir}]}
    ],
    {x, pos}
  ],
  Frame -> True, PlotStyle -> AbsoluteThickness[1]
 ]
]
```

If you want, I can also show you how to make the tangent update interactively with a slider (`Manipulate`).

