The main issue is that `Control` has `HoldFirst`, so building it purely from extracted list elements is trickier than for things like `Slider` or `SetterBar`.

A simple way to do this is:

- store the candidate controls as data
- choose which indices are already used
- generate the remaining controls with `Map`/`Table`
- use `Control` directly inside `DynamicModule`

Here is a working version based on your example:

```wl
DynamicModule[{fC = 1, sC = 2, lC, allC},
  allC = {
    {{c1, 1, "c1"}, {1, 2}},
    {{c2, 10, "c2"}, {10, 20}},
    {{c3, 100, "c3"}, {100, 200}},
    {{c4, 1000, "c4"}, {1000, 2000}}
  };
  lC = Range[Length[allC]];

  Column[{
    Control[{{fC, 1, "First"}, lC}],
    Control[{{sC, 2, "Second"}, Complement[lC, {fC}]}],

    Dynamic@Column[
      Map[
        With[{item = allC[[#]]},
          Control[item[[1]], item[[2]]]
        ] &,
        Complement[lC, {fC, sC}]
      ]
    ],

    Dynamic@Column[
      Table[
        With[{item = allC[[i]]},
          Control[item[[1]], item[[2]]]
        ],
        {i, lC}
      ]
    ]
  }]
]
```

If you want the controls to correspond to the remaining variables only, the key part is:

```wl
Map[
  With[{item = allC[[#]]},
    Control[item[[1]], item[[2]]]
  ] &,
  Complement[lC, {fC, sC}]
]
```

A few notes:

- `Complement` is cleaner here than `DeleteCases`
- `With[{item = ...}]` is important so each loop iteration captures its own data
- `Dynamic@Column[...]` makes the list of controls refresh when `fC` or `sC` changes

If you'd like, I can also rewrite this so the variable names are handled symbolically as in your original `c1`, `c2`, `c3`, `c4` version.

Yes. For a closed curve given as an ordered list of points, you can “fill” a curve in the sense of finding where it intersects a plane by detecting sign changes of the plane equation along the sampled points.

For your example plane z = 0.5, look at the sequence

- z_i - 0.5

and find where it changes sign between consecutive points. Those intervals bracket the crossings, and then you can linearly interpolate to get the actual intersection points.

A simple Mathematica approach is:

```wl
dt = Table[{Sin[3 t] Cos[t], Sin[t] Sin[5 t], Sin[2 t]}, {t, -10, 10, 0.005}];

crossings = Position[Partition[Last /@ dt - 0.5, 2, 1], {_?Positive, _?Negative} | {_?Negative, _?Positive}];
```

This gives index pairs where the curve crosses the plane. Then compute the intersection points:

```wl
intersections = Table[
  Module[{p1 = dt[[i]], p2 = dt[[i + 1]], s1, s2, λ},
    s1 = p1[[3]] - 0.5;
    s2 = p2[[3]] - 0.5;
    λ = s1/(s1 - s2);
    p1 + λ (p2 - p1)
  ],
  {i, crossings[[All, 1]], crossings[[All, 1]]}
];
```

A more compact version for the plane z = c is:

```wl
intersections = Table[
  With[{p1 = dt[[i]], p2 = dt[[i + 1]]},
    p1 + (0.5 - p1[[3]])/(p2[[3]] - p1[[3]]) (p2 - p1)
  ],
  {i, Flatten[crossings]}
];
```

If you want a general plane, define the plane by a function f[{x,y,z}] = 0. Then find sign changes in `f /@ dt`.

For example, for the plane x + y + z - 1 = 0:

```wl
f[p_] := Total[p] - 1;

idx = Position[Partition[f /@ dt, 2, 1], {_?Positive, _?Negative} | {_?Negative, _?Positive}];
```

Then interpolate as above using `f[p1]` and `f[p2]`.

