`0x1A` is being treated as end-of-file because of the old DOS/Windows EOF convention.

In Mathematica/Wolfram Language, the relevant setting is:

```wl
$IgnoreEOF = True
```

This tells `Read`/`OpenRead` to ignore `EndOfFile` markers in the data stream and continue reading past `0x1A`.

Example:

```wl
$IgnoreEOF = True;
stream = OpenRead[filepath];
txt = Read[stream, String];
Close[stream];
```

If you want to be extra safe, you can localize it:

```wl
Block[{$IgnoreEOF = True},
  stream = OpenRead[filepath];
  txt = Read[stream, String];
  Close[stream];
]
```

If `Read[stream, String]` still isn’t suitable for your splitting task, you may want to read in chunks and process the stream incrementally, but for the specific `EndOfFile` issue, `$IgnoreEOF = True` is the fix.

If you’re working in Mathematica, the usual approach is:

1. Import the CSV as text
2. Remove comment lines
3. Split/parse the remaining CSV data

If comments are on lines starting with something like `#`, you can do:

```mathematica
text = Import[filename, "Text"];
lines = StringSplit[text, "\n"];
dataLines = Select[lines, Not@StringStartsQ[StringTrim[#], "#"] &];
data = ImportString[StringRiffle[dataLines, "\n"], "CSV"];
```

If you want to remove blank lines too:

```mathematica
dataLines = Select[lines, 
  StringTrim[#] =!= "" && Not@StringStartsQ[StringTrim[#], "#"] &
];
```

If your file has comment sections mixed with data sections, and the comments have a different pattern, you can filter on that pattern instead.

For example, if comment lines are those that don’t match CSV rows with commas:

```mathematica
dataLines = Select[lines, StringContainsQ[#, ","] &];
data = ImportString[StringRiffle[dataLines, "\n"], "CSV"];
```

