Yes — you can use `Blend` directly inside the graphics, and if you want to “compile” the color mapping into a reusable color function, define it as a function of the value and pass that into `Blend`.

For your case, a simple approach is:

```wl
distance = {0.245, -0.235, 0.053, -0.048, -0.128, -0.007, -0.075, -0.067, -0.005, 0.082};

colorFunction = Function[x,
  Blend[
    {{-Max[Abs[distance]], Red},
     {0, LightRed},
     {0, LightGreen},
     {+Max[Abs[distance]], Green}},
    x
  ]
];
```

Then use it in your graphics:

```wl
Show[
  (Function[attributes,
     Graphics[{
       colorFunction[distance[[attributes]]],
       Rectangle[
         {If[distance[[attributes]] < 0, distance[[attributes]]*10, 0], attributes - 1},
         {If[distance[[attributes]] < 0, 0, distance[[attributes]]*10], attributes}
       ]
     },
     PlotRange -> {{-5, 5}, {0, 10}},
     Epilog -> {White, Line[{{0, 0}, {0, 11}}]}
   ]]) /@ Range[10],
  Frame -> True
]
```

If you want the color function itself to be more compact, you can also write it inline:

```wl
Blend[{{-m, Red}, {0, LightRed}, {0, LightGreen}, {m, Green}}, x]
```

where `m = Max[Abs[distance]]`.

A slightly cleaner version:

```wl
m = Max[Abs[distance]];
cf[x_] := Blend[{{-m, Red}, {0, LightRed}, {0, LightGreen}, {m, Green}}, x];
```

Then:

```wl
Show[
  Table[
    Graphics[{
      cf[distance[[i]]],
      Rectangle[
        {If[distance[[i]] < 0, distance[[i]]*10, 0], i - 1},
        {If[distance[[i]] < 0, 0, distance[[i]]*10], i}
      ]
    },
    PlotRange -> {{-5, 5}, {0, 10}},
