Yes — the basic problem is that `Histogram3D` axis/frame labels are still 3D text, so they rotate with the viewpoint and quickly become unreadable.

A practical workaround is to replace the labels with textured polygons: rasterize the label text into an image, then map that image onto a flat polygon in the x-y plane. That keeps the text visually stable regardless of rotation.

Here’s the idea from the resolved thread, adapted:

```wl
histo = Histogram3D[
  RandomVariate[UniformDistribution[{0, 10}], {500, 2}],
  ChartElementFunction -> "GradientScaleCube"
];

labels1 = Rasterize[
  Style[Column[DictionaryLookup["a*"][[;; 10]], Dividers -> All], 
    FontFamily -> "Calibri"],
  ImageSize -> {{10^6}, {300}}, RasterSize -> 300
];

labels2 = Rasterize[
  Style[Column[DictionaryLookup["z*"][[-10 ;;]], Dividers -> All], 
    FontFamily -> "Calibri"],
  ImageSize -> {{10^6}, {300}}, RasterSize -> 300
];

w1 = ImageDimensions[labels1][[1]]*10./300;
w2 = ImageDimensions[labels2][[1]]*10./300;

Show[
  histo,
  Graphics3D[{
    Texture[labels1],
    Polygon[{{-w1 - 1, 0, 0}, {-1, 0, 0}, {-1, 10, 0}, {-w1 - 1, 10, 0}},
      VertexTextureCoordinates -> {{0, 0}, {1, 0}, {1, 1}, {0, 1}}
    ],
    Texture[labels2],
    Polygon[{{0, 11 + w2, 0}, {0, 11, 0}, {10, 11, 0}, {10, 11 + w2, 0}},
      VertexTextureCoordinates -> {{0, 0}, {1, 0}, {1, 1}, {0, 1}}
    ]
  }],
  PlotRange -> All,
  Boxed -> False,
  BoxRatios -> {1, 1, 0.4},
  Axes -> {False, False, True},
  FaceGrids -> {{{0, 0, -1}, {Range[0, 10], Range[0, 10]}}}
]
```

Why this helps:
- The labels are no longer true 3D text.
- They are images fixed to polygons in the plot plane.
- So they don’t twist or tilt as you rotate the histogram.

