For a polyline, the clean way is to parameterize it by arc length and then sample equal arc-length positions along it.

If you already have the polyline as a list of points, say

```mathematica
pts = {{x1, y1}, {x2, y2}, ..., {xm, ym}};
```

you can do this:

```mathematica
equidistantPointsOnPolyline[pts_, n_] := Module[
  {segLen, cumLen, totalLen, targets, lengths, interp, distFun},

  segLen = Norm /@ Differences[pts];
  cumLen = Prepend[Accumulate[segLen], 0];
  totalLen = Last[cumLen];
  targets = Subdivide[0, totalLen, n - 1];

  interp = Interpolation[
    Transpose[{cumLen, pts}],
    Method -> "Spline"
  ];

  (* This works only if the polyline is reasonably smooth.
     For exact piecewise-linear sampling, use the function below instead. *)
  interp /@ targets
]
```

But for an actual polyline, you usually want exact points on the segments, not spline interpolation. Here is the exact piecewise-linear version:

```mathematica
equidistantPointsOnPolyline[pts_, n_] := Module[
  {segLen, cumLen, totalLen, targets, segIndex, t},

  segLen = Norm /@ Differences[pts];
  cumLen = Prepend[Accumulate[segLen], 0];
  totalLen = Last[cumLen];
  targets = Subdivide[0, totalLen, n - 1];

  Table[
    segIndex = First@FirstPosition[cumLen, _?(# >= s &)] - 1;
    If[segIndex < 1, segIndex = 1];
    t = (s - cumLen[[segIndex]])/segLen[[segIndex]];
    (1 - t) pts[[segIndex]] + t pts[[segIndex + 1]],
    {s, targets}
  ]
]
```

Example:

```mathematica
poly = {{0, 0}, {2, 1}, {5, 1}, {7, 4}};
pts2 = equidistantPointsOnPolyline[poly, 10];

Graphics[
 {Thick, Line[poly], Red, PointSize[Large], Point[pts2]},
 Frame -> True
]
```

This gives 10 points equally spaced along the polyline’s length.

If you want, I can also give a version that works on a `Line[...]` object directly, or one that returns points including/excluding endpoints.

If you want the same information as `Tally`, but split into two separate lists in a way that is more compiler-friendly, the usual pattern is:

- one list for the unique items
- one list for the corresponding counts

For example, from:

```wl
Tally[...]
```

you want to produce something like:

```wl
{items, counts}
```

instead of:

```wl
{{item1, count1}, {item2, count2}, ...}
```

A compiled-friendly approach is to accumulate the two parts separately. In the previously resolved thread, this was done with `Internal\`Bag` inside `Compile`.

Here is the basic idea:

```wl
tally = Compile[
  {{lst, _Real, 2}, {delta, _Real, 0}, {j, _Integer, 0}},
  Block[{
    sorted = Sort@Round[lst/(delta^j)],
    itemBag = Internal`Bag[],
    countBag = Internal`Bag[],
    lastItem, count = 0
  },
   lastItem = First[sorted];
   Do[
    If[i == lastItem,
      ++count,
      Internal`StuffBag[itemBag, lastItem];
      Internal`StuffBag[countBag, count];
      lastItem = i;
      count = 1
    ],
    {i, Rest[sorted]}
   ];
   Internal`StuffBag[itemBag, lastItem];
   Internal`StuffBag[countBag, count];
   {Internal`BagPart[itemBag, All], Internal`BagPart[countBag, All]}
  ]
];
```

This returns:

```wl
{items, counts}
```

instead of a ragged `Tally` result.

