`NDSolve` is complaining because `ListConvolve` expects a concrete list/array, but `x[t]` is still an unknown symbolic quantity during the setup phase. So this part is the problem:

```mathematica
ListConvolve[A, x[t], 1]
```

You need to wrap that convolution in a helper function that only evaluates when `x[t]` is numeric.

Example:

```mathematica
A = {{1, 2, 3}, {4, 5, 6}, {7, 8, 9}};
B = {{9, 8, 7}, {6, 5, 4}, {3, 2, 1}};
u = {{10, 11, 12, 1}, {20, 21, 22, 1}, {66, 77, 88, 1}};
Ioff = 1;

fun[xVal_] /; MatrixQ[xVal, NumericQ] := ListConvolve[A, xVal, 1];

sol = NDSolveValue[
  {
    x'[t] == -x[t] + fun[x[t]] + ListConvolve[B, u, 1] + Ioff,
    x[0] == u
  },
  x, {t, 0, 2}
];
```

Then evaluate it with:

```mathematica
sol[0.2]
```

Why this works:
- `x[t]` is symbolic when `NDSolve` first parses the equation.
- `fun[...]` delays the convolution until `x[t]` becomes a numeric matrix during integration.
- `MatrixQ[xVal, NumericQ]` ensures the helper only acts on actual numeric arrays.

One note: this system may grow very quickly for larger `t`, so if you get instability or huge values, it may be due to the model itself rather than the syntax.

The issue is that `Manipulate` does not always preserve the values/definitions you created in the input cell the way you expect when the notebook is opened fresh. In particular, your code relies on `Sequence1` and `Function1` being defined before the dynamic content is initialized.

The fix is to put those definitions in the `Initialization` option of `Manipulate`, so they are guaranteed to exist when the interface is built.

Example:

```mathematica
Manipulate[
 Grid[{
   {"Function", "Sequence of Outputs"},
   {data = List[Transpose[{
        Table[Sequence1[n][[sss]], {n, 1, t}],
        Table[Function1[Sequence1[n][[sss]]][[hhh]], {n, 1, t}]
      }]];
    ListPlot[data, PlotRange -> {{-1, 1}, {0, 5}}, AspectRatio -> 1/1, ImageSize -> Full],
    ListPlot[Table[Function1[Sequence1[n][[sss]]][[hhh]], {n, 1, t}],
      PlotRange -> Automatic, ImageSize -> Full]
   },
   {"Close-Up", "Tabular Values"},
   (* rest of your code *)
  }],
 {{sss, 3, "sequence"}, {1 -> "1/n", 2 -> "(-1)^n/n", 3 -> "1/Sqrt[n]"}, SetterBar},
 {{hhh, 3, "f(x)"}, {1 -> "(x-2)^2", 2 -> "2-x^2", 3 -> "2^x"}, SetterBar},
 {t, 1, 100, 1},
 Initialization :> (
   Sequence1[n_] := {1/n, (-1)^n/n, 1/Sqrt[n]};
   Function1[x_] := {(x - 2)^2, 2 - x^2, 2^x};
 )
]
```

Why this helps:
- `Initialization` runs before the `Manipulate` is displayed.
- Your helper functions are then always defined, even after reopening the notebook.
- Without it, the dynamic may evaluate before those definitions are available, causing blank or incorrect plots.

Also, it’s generally better to avoid using assignments like `data = ...` directly inside the displayed expression unless you really need them. But the main problem here is the missing initialization.

If you want, I can also help rewrite the code to make it cleaner and more robust.

