The usual way to monitor file changes in Wolfram Language is to use a `ScheduledTask`, not `Dynamic`.

A `Dynamic` will reevaluate based on its tracked symbols, but in your case it’s still getting triggered on the refresh interval, so your function runs repeatedly whether the file changed or not.

Use a task that periodically checks the file’s modification time, and only runs your import/processing code when that timestamp changes.

Example:

```wl
fileName = "testfile.csv";
lastModified = None;

updatedQ[] := Module[{modTime = FileDate[fileName, "Modification"]},
  If[modTime =!= lastModified,
    lastModified = modTime;
    True,
    False
  ]
];

task = CreateScheduledTask[
  If[updatedQ[],
    data = Import[fileName, "CSV"];
    f[data]
  ],
  {2, Infinity}
];

StartScheduledTask[task];
```

### Notes
- It checks every 2 seconds here, but you can change that interval.
- `f[data]` only runs when the file’s modification time changes.
- You can stop or remove the task later:

```wl
StopScheduledTask[task]
RemoveScheduledTask[task]
```

