In Mathematica 9, PlotLegends can handle combined plots, but for mixed object types like Plot and ListPlot, the cleanest route is usually to combine them in a single plotting function (for example ListLogLogPlot / ListLinePlot / Show) and then attach a single legend using PlotLegends.

The main limitations are:

- legends are easier if all plotted elements are treated as one graphics object
- custom markers and line styles are supported
- for different legend entries, you need to explicitly provide matching legend markers/styles

A corrected version of the example you showed would look like this:

```wl
MakeExponent[x_Integer] := "\*SuperscriptBox[10," <> ToString[x] <> "]";
tick = {10^#, MakeExponent[#]} & /@ Range[-10, 10, 1];

A = {1/#, #^2} & /@ Range[100];
B = {1/#, #^1.5 - 1} & /@ Range[100];
A1 = {1/#, #^2} & /@ Range[100];
B1 = {1/#, #^1.5 - 1} & /@ Range[100];

ALegend = Style["Legend A", 12, Black, FontFamily -> "Courier"];
BLegend = Style["Legend B", 12, Black, FontFamily -> "Helvetica"];
A1Legend = Style["Legend A1", 12, Black, FontFamily -> "Courier"];
B1Legend = Style["Legend B1", 12, Black, FontFamily -> "Courier"];

AMarker = Style["\[FilledCircle]", 10, Orange];
BMarker = Style["\[FilledDiamond]", 10, Purple];
A1Marker = Style["\[LongDash]", 15, Blue];
B1Marker = Style["\[LongDash]", 15, Red];

XLabel = Style["x", 17];
YLabel = Style["y", 17];

pic1 = ListLogLogPlot[
  {A, B, A1, B1},
  Axes -> False,
  Frame -> True,
  Joined -> {False, False, True, True},
  PlotStyle -> {Blue, Red, Blue, Red},
  FrameLabel -> {XLabel, YLabel},
  PlotMarkers -> {AMarker, BMarker, None, None},
  PlotLegends -> Placed[
    PointLegend[
      {ALegend, BLegend, A1Legend, B1Legend},
      LegendMarkers -> {AMarker, BMarker, A1Marker, B1Marker},
      LegendMarkerSize -> {30, 10}
    ],
    {0.8, 0.7}
  ],
  FrameTicks -> {{tick, None}, {tick, None}},
  FrameTicksStyle -> 16
];
```

A few notes:

1. PlotStyle must match all plotted datasets
   - If you pass 4 datasets, PlotStyle should normally have 4 entries.
   - Same for PlotMarkers if you want different markers.

2. Joined controls line vs point behavior
   - `Joined -> {False, False, True, True}` means first two are points, last two are lines.

3. PointLegend is used here because the first two are point datasets
   - If you want a combined legend for lines and points, you can still use PointLegend, but you must supply markers for each entry.
   - In newer versions, `LineLegend` and `PointLegend` can be mixed more flexibly.

4. If you want one plot from Plot and one from ListPlot
   - You can also do:
     ```wl
     Show[
       Plot[..., PlotStyle -> ...],
       ListPlot[..., PlotMarkers -> ...],
       PlotLegends -> ...
     ]
     ```
   - But in older versions, legends often work more reliably when generated inside the plotting function rather than on `Show`.

