For a parameterized curve, the tangent vector at parameter value t0 is just the derivative of the position vector with respect to the parameter, evaluated at t0.

If the curve is

r(t) = <x(t), y(t)>   or   r(t) = <x(t), y(t), z(t)>,

then the tangent vector at t = t0 is

r'(t0) = <x'(t0), y'(t0)>   or   <x'(t0), y'(t0), z'(t0)>.

In Mathematica, that is typically as simple as:

r[t_] := {x[t], y[t]};
t0 = (* your parameter value *);
tangentVector = D[r[t], t] /. t -> t0

If you want the tangent line as well, use the point r[t0] and direction r'(t0):

r[t0] + s r'(t0)

where s is a new parameter.

For example, for a curve defined by x(t), y(t):

r[t_] := {Cos[t], Sin[t]};
t0 = Pi/4;
pt = r[t0];
vec = D[r[t], t] /. t -> t0;
line[s_] := pt + s vec

