Yes — for this kind of “simple macro/meta-programming” task in Mathematica, the usual pattern is:

1. let `CoxModelFit` do the statistics, and  
2. build the forest plot yourself from low-level graphics primitives.

That’s essentially what the resolved thread shows. There isn’t a built-in forest-plot command in Mathematica 9, but it’s quite feasible to make one.

A compact approach is:

```mathematica
larynx = ExampleData[{"Statistics", "LarynxCancer"}];
x = larynx[[All, {1, 3}]];
e = EventData[larynx[[All, 2]], larynx[[All, 5]]];

mod = CoxModelFit[{x, e}, {stage, age}, {stage, age},
  NominalVariables -> stage
];
```

Then extract the estimates and confidence intervals:

```mathematica
rr = mod["RelativeRisk"];
rrci = mod["RelativeRiskConfidenceIntervals"];
vars = mod["BasisFunctions"] /. HoldPattern[DiscreteIndicator[s_, i_, _]] :> s[i];
```

And plot them manually:

```mathematica
forestPlot[mod_CoxModel] := Block[
  {rr, rrci, vars, p},
  rr = mod["RelativeRisk"];
  rrci = mod["RelativeRiskConfidenceIntervals"];
  vars = mod["BasisFunctions"] /. HoldPattern[DiscreteIndicator[s_, i_, _]] :> s[i];
  p = Length[vars];

  Graphics[
    Join[
      {Dashed, Line[{{1, 0}, {1, p + 1}}]},
      MapThread[
        {Thick, Line[{{#1[[1]], #2}, {#1[[2]], #2}}]} &,
        {rrci, Range[p]}
      ],
      MapThread[
        {EdgeForm[{Thick, Black}], FaceForm[White],
         Polygon[{{-.2 + #1, #2}, {#1, .1 + #2}, {.2 + #1, #2}, {#1, -.1 + #2}}]} &,
        {rr, Range[p]}
      ]
    ],
    AspectRatio -> 1,
    Frame -> True,
    PlotRangePadding -> {1, 0},
    FrameTicks -> {Automatic, Transpose[{Range[p], vars}], None, None},
    GridLines -> Automatic,
    GridLinesStyle -> White
  ]
]
```

A few notes:

- The vertical line at 1 is the “no effect” reference line.
- The horizontal segments are the confidence intervals.
- The diamond markers are the relative risks.
- Because this uses primitive `Graphics`, you can customize labels, colors, spacing, axis style, etc. pretty freely.

