`ListVectorPlot3D` is not the right tool here if every vector starts at the origin. It is meant for sampling a vector field over 3D space, not for drawing a bunch of arrows from one point.

Use `Graphics3D` with `Arrow` instead.

### Convert spherical to Cartesian
If your data are `{r, theta, phi}` points, convert them like this:

```wolfram
spherical = Table[
  With[{x = RandomReal[]},
    {x, ArcCos[x], 2 Pi x}
  ],
  {3}
];

cartesian = CoordinateTransform["Spherical" -> "Cartesian", spherical]
```

### Build arrows from the origin
Since all arrows start at `{0,0,0}`:

```wolfram
arrows = Arrow[{{0, 0, 0}, #}] & /@ cartesian;
Graphics3D[arrows]
```

### If you want the data in the right shape
`ListVectorPlot3D` expects something like:

```wolfram
{{x, y, z, vx, vy, vz}, ...}
```

or a structured array representing a vector field on a grid, not

```wolfram
{{{0,0,0},{vx,vy,vz}}, ...}
```

So your `cords` is close for arrows, but not in the format `ListVectorPlot3D` wants.

### Better way to generate your sample data
Instead of `AppendTo`, use `Table`:

```wolfram
spherical = Table[
  With[{x = RandomReal[]},
    {x, ArcCos[x], 2 Pi x}
  ],
  {100}
];
```

Then:

```wolfram
cartesian = CoordinateTransform["Spherical" -> "Cartesian", spherical];
Graphics3D[Arrow[{{0, 0, 0}, #}] & /@ cartesian]
```

If you want, I can also show how to make a true `ListVectorPlot3D`-compatible vector field from your data.

