data = Import[newestFile, "Table"];
```

Or more compactly:

```wolfram
newestFile = First @ Reverse @ SortBy[FileNames["*.dat"], FileDate[#, "Modification"] &];
```

Yes — you can do this with a polling loop using Dynamic, but for “run code when a new file appears” a ScheduledTask-style monitor is usually simpler.

A good pattern is:

1. keep track of the current file list
2. periodically refresh it
3. compare old vs new
4. process any newly added file(s)

For example:

```wl
files = FileNames["*", "C:\\path\\to\\dir"];
active = False;
nb = EvaluationNotebook[];
timeRes = .2;

Button["Monitor",
 task = RunScheduledTask[
   active = True;
   old = files;
   files = FileNames["*", "C:\\path\\to\\dir"];
   If[old =!= files,
    newFiles = Complement[files, old];
    NotebookWrite[nb, {
      Cell["New files are:", "Output"],
      Cell[BoxData @ ToBoxes[newFiles], "Output"]
      }];
    (* put your file-processing code here *)
    ],
   timeRes],
 Enabled -> Dynamic@Not@active
]

Button["Stop",
 RemoveScheduledTask@task;
 active = False,
 Enabled -> Dynamic@active
]

Dynamic[Column@files, UpdateInterval -> timeRes]
```

If you want to import the chronologically newest file specifically, use file modification times instead of just alphabetical ordering:

```wl
dir = "C:\\path\\to\\dir";

newestFile[] := First @ ReverseSortBy[
   FileNames["*", dir],
   FileDate
];

Dynamic[newestFile[], UpdateInterval -> .2]
```

And to import it:

```wl
Import[newestFile[]]
```

If you want to avoid re-importing the same file repeatedly, store the last processed file and only act when it changes.

Example:

```wl
dir = "C:\\path\\to\\dir";
lastFile = None;

Dynamic[
 Module[{f = First @ ReverseSortBy[FileNames["*", dir], FileDate]},
  If[f =!= lastFile,
   lastFile = f;
   Print["New newest file: ", f];
   Print[Import[f]];
   ];
  ],
 UpdateInterval -> .2
]
```

So in short:

- Dynamic can refresh the display
- ScheduledTask is better if you want the kernel to actively monitor and run code
- use `FileDate` + `ReverseSortBy` if you need the newest file by timestamp

