Use Block, not Module.

Module gives fresh local symbols like var1$123, so ToExpression["var1"] won’t refer to the local var1 you assigned inside the function. Block temporarily rebinds the existing symbol names, which is what you want here.

Example:

func[var_String] := Block[{var1, var2},
  var1 = "some words";
  var2 = "other words";
  ToExpression[var]
]

func["var1"]
(* "some words" *)

So the fix is to replace Module with Block.

You don’t need to convert `m` into `n` or `l` by name. In Mathematica, if `m`, `n`, `l`, etc. are lists, you can store them in a single list and process them by position.

For example:

```mathematica
data = {m, n, l};
```

Then you can build your matrix row by row:

```mathematica
xmatrixx = Table[0, {Length[data]}, {100}];

Do[
  xmatrixx[[i, 1 ;; Length[Flatten[data[[i]]]]]] = Flatten[data[[i]]],
  {i, Length[data]}
]
```

But the cleaner approach is not to create an empty matrix first. Instead:

```mathematica
xmatrixx = (Join[Flatten[#], ConstantArray[0, 100 - Length[Flatten[#]]]] &) /@ {m, n, l};
```

If you have many files/lists, put them all in one list:

```mathematica
data = {m, n, l, ...};
xmatrixx = (Join[Flatten[#], ConstantArray[0, 100 - Length[Flatten[#]]]] &) /@ data;
```

If your data come from `.dat` files, use their filenames:

```mathematica
files = {"m.dat", "n.dat", "l.dat"};
xmatrixx = (With[{imported = Flatten@Import[#]},
    Join[imported, ConstantArray[0, 100 - Length[imported]]]
  ] &) /@ files;
```

So the key idea is: instead of trying to “change `mflatten` into `n`”, store all lists/files in a container and loop over them.

Yes — this is a perspective correction problem. In Mathematica, the usual way to do it is to define 4 corresponding points on the skewed image and on the desired “flat” rectangle, then apply a perspective transformation.

A simple workflow is:

1. Pick four corners of the mural in the photo.
2. Map them to the corners of a rectangle.
3. Use `ImagePerspectiveTransformation`.

Example:

```wl
img = Import["yourImage.jpg"];

src = {
   {x1, y1},   (* top-left corner in the photo *)
   {x2, y2},   (* top-right *)
   {x3, y3},   (* bottom-right *)
   {x4, y4}    (* bottom-left *)
};

dst = {
   {0, 0},
   {w, 0},
   {w, h},
   {0, h}
};

t = FindGeometricTransform[src, dst, "Transformation" -> "Perspective"][[2]];

rectified = ImagePerspectiveTransformation[img, t];
```

If you don’t know the points exactly, you can use `Locator` or the Drawing Tools to click them manually.

For an interactive version, you can do something like:

```wl
img = ImageResize[img, 300];
id = ImageDimensions[img];

Manipulate[
 Row[{
   Show[img,
    Graphics[{
      {FaceForm[None], EdgeForm[{Dashed, Thick, Blue}], Polygon[pt]},
      {Red, PointSize[Large], Point[pt]}
    }]
   ],
   Image[
    ImagePerspectiveTransformation[
     img,
     FindGeometricTransform[
       pt,
       {{0, 0}, {id[[1]], 0}, {id[[1]], id[[2]]}, {0, id[[2]]}},
       "Transformation" -> "Perspective"
     ][[2]],
     DataRange -> Full,
     PlotRange -> All
    ],
    ImageSize -> 300
   ]
 }],
 {{pt, {{id[[1]]/4, id[[2]]/4}, {3 id[[1]]/4, id[[2]]/4}, {3 id[[1]]/4, 3 id[[2]]/4}, {id[[1]]/4, 3 id[[2]]/4}}}, Locator}
]
```

A few notes:

- This works best when the mural is actually planar.
- If the photo was taken with strong lens distortion, you may need to correct that first.
- If the mural surface is not perfectly flat, no transformation will fully recover a true front-on view.

