The issue is not `OpenWrite`; it’s that `Read` leaves the external-command stream open.

When you use:

```wl
Read["!" <> comm <> " < " <> First@in <> " > " <> First@out]
```

Mathematica opens a stream to the command, reads from it, but does not automatically close it. Repeating this in a loop eventually exhausts handles, which is why errors start appearing at a fixed iteration count.

Use one of these fixes:

### Option 1: Open and close explicitly
```wl
runThrough[comm_String, s_String] := Block[{in = OpenWrite[], out = OpenWrite[], strm},
  WriteString[in, s <> "\n"];
  Close /@ {in, out};
  strm = OpenRead["!" <> comm <> " < " <> First@in <> " > " <> First@out];
  Read[strm];
  Close[strm];
  DeleteFile /@ {First@in, First@out}
]
```

### Option 2: Use `ReadList`, which auto-closes
```wl
runThrough[comm_String, s_String] := Block[{in = OpenWrite[], out = OpenWrite[]},
  WriteString[in, s <> "\n"];
  Close /@ {in, out};
  First@ReadList["!" <> comm <> " < " <> First@in <> " > " <> First@out, Expression, 1];
  DeleteFile /@ {First@in, First@out}
]
```

### Why this happens
- `Read` is designed for reading in pieces, so it does not auto-close.
- `ReadList` reads everything it needs and closes automatically.

So the fix is to close the stream returned by `OpenRead`, or switch to `ReadList`.

When `PlotLegends` is used together with `Show`, it often behaves badly because `Show` combines graphics after the legend has already been attached.

The usual fix in Mathematica 8 is to use `ShowLegend` from `PlotLegends`` instead of `PlotLegends` directly.

Example:

```mathematica
eqns = {Sin[3 Tanh[#]], Sin[Erf[#]]} &;
colors = {Blue, Red};
legends = TraditionalForm /@ eqns[x];

plot1 = Plot[Evaluate@eqns[x], {x, 0, π},
  PlotStyle -> colors,
  Filling -> {1 -> {{2}, LightGreen}}
];

plot2 = ListPlot[
  Select[RandomReal[{0, Pi}, {1000, 2}],
    IntervalMemberQ[Interval[Sort[eqns[First@#]]], Last@#] &
  ],
  PlotStyle -> Darker@Green
];

ShowLegend[
  Show[plot1, plot2],
  {
    {Graphics[{#1, Thick, Line[{{0, 0}, {1, 0}}]}], #2} & @@@
      Transpose[{colors, legends}]
  },
  LegendPosition -> {-0.65, -0.5},
  LegendSpacing -> 0,
  LegendShadow -> None,
  LegendSize -> 0.6
]
```

### Key point
- `PlotLegends` works best when it is applied directly to the plotting function.
- If you need to combine multiple graphics with `Show`, use `ShowLegend` so the legend is added to the final combined graphic.

