- an example with multiple option categories,
- or a full `LinearModelFit`-style object with property queries.

If you want to work with several input cells “at once” in a grouped notebook, the main trick is that a cell cannot directly ask for its parent cell/group. Instead, you move the notebook selection to the relevant cell and then inspect that cell’s properties.

A working approach is:

```wl
firstcell[nb_] := (
  SelectionMove[nb, All, CellGroup];
  SelectionMove[nb, Before, CellGroup];
  SelectionMove[nb, Next, Cell]
)

parentcell[] := Module[{cellstyle, nb},
  nb = EvaluationNotebook[];
  SelectionMove[nb, All, EvaluationCell];
  firstcell[nb];
  cellstyle = NotebookRead[nb][[2]];

  (* if the current cell is an input cell, skip the input/output pair *)
  If[cellstyle == "Input",
    SelectionMove[nb, All, CellGroup];
    firstcell[nb]
  ]
]
```

Then you can access options of the parent cell like this:

```wl
parentcell[];
CurrentValue[NotebookSelection[EvaluationNotebook[]], CellTags]
```

If you want to go up until a specific grouping style, for example `"Section"`:

```wl
parentcell[style_String] := Module[{nb},
  nb = EvaluationNotebook[];
  SelectionMove[nb, All, EvaluationCell];
  firstcell[nb];

  While[NotebookRead[nb][[2]] =!= style,
    SelectionMove[nb, All, CellGroup];
    firstcell[nb]
  ]
]
```

Example:

```wl
parentcell["Section"];
CurrentValue[NotebookSelection[EvaluationNotebook[]], CellTags]
```

Notes:
- This works by moving the notebook selection, since the kernel doesn’t directly know a cell’s parent.
- The simple version has some odd behavior if the cell has no real hierarchy beyond input/output grouping.
- If you’re trying to parallelize evaluation of several cells simultaneously, keep in mind that notebook selection is a front-end operation and is not thread-safe in the way normal kernel computation is.

If you want, I can also show how to adapt this to:
1. get the parent cell without changing the visible selection, or
2. process multiple input cells in a grouped notebook programmatically.

