If your “sets” are really multisets/lists with multiplicities, then subtraction means:

for each element, keep count = max(count in a − count in b, 0)

In Mathematica/Wolfram Language, a clean way is to use Tally and rebuild the list:

```wl
a = {1, 1, 2, 1, 1, 3};
b = {1, 5, 5, 1};

With[{du = DeleteDuplicates@Join[a, b]},
  Join @@ ConstantArray @@@
    Transpose@{
      du,
      Abs[Subtract[
        Tally[Join[du, a]],
        Tally[Join[du, b]]
      ][[All, 2]]]
    }
]
```

Result:

```wl
{1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 5}
```

This gives the symmetric difference of the multisets, i.e. the multiset version of subtraction on multiplicities.

If you only want the one-sided difference a \ b, then conceptually it’s just the elements of a with counts reduced by b’s counts. In WL, a simple idiom is to use counts and rebuild from them.

If you want a random snowflake in Mathematica, a simple way is to build one arm randomly and then copy it with 6-fold symmetry.

