Yes. A common workaround is to move the notebook selection to the target “parent” cell, then read or modify its options via `CurrentValue` / `SetOptions`.

For example, if you want to get the parent cell and inspect one of its options:

```wl
parentcell[] := Module[{cellstyle, nb},
  nb = EvaluationNotebook[];
  SelectionMove[nb, All, EvaluationCell];
  firstcell[nb];
  cellstyle = NotebookRead[nb][[2]];

  (* if the current cell is the input cell, move to the enclosing group *)
  If[cellstyle == "Input",
    SelectionMove[nb, All, CellGroup];
    firstcell[nb]
  ]
]

firstcell[nb_] := (
  SelectionMove[nb, All, CellGroup];
  SelectionMove[nb, Before, CellGroup];
  SelectionMove[nb, Next, Cell]
)
```

Then you can access the options of that cell:

```wl
parentcell[];
CurrentValue[NotebookSelection[EvaluationNotebook[]], CellTags]
```

If you want to go up the hierarchy until you find a cell with a particular style, you can use:

```wl
parentcell[style_String] := Module[{nb},
  nb = EvaluationNotebook[];
  SelectionMove[nb, All, EvaluationCell];
  firstcell[nb];

  While[NotebookRead[nb][[2]] =!= style,
    SelectionMove[nb, All, CellGroup];
    firstcell[nb]
  ]
]
```

So in short: yes, but not directly from the other cell’s contents alone—you typically navigate through the notebook structure and then use `CurrentValue` or related functions on the selected cell.

You can do this by splitting the plot into two pieces: one with filling up to your cutoff line, and one without filling after it. Then draw the vertical line only up to the curve height.

Here’s a compact version based on the resolved thread:

```wl
Manipulate[
  dist = PDF[NormalDistribution[5, 1], #] &;

  p1 = Plot[dist@x, {x, 0, l},
    Filling -> Axis,
    PlotRange -> {{0, 10}, All}
  ];

  p2 = Plot[dist@x, {x, l, 10},
    Filling -> None,
    PlotRange -> {{0, 10}, All}
  ];

  Show[
    p1, p2,
    Epilog -> {
      Orange,
      Line[{{l, 0}, {l, dist@l}}]
    },
    PlotLabel -> Style[
      Row[{"l = ", NumberForm[dist@l, {Infinity, 2}]}],
      20
    ]
  ],
  {{l, 5.}, 0, 10}
]
```

Notes:
- `Filling -> Axis` shades under the curve down to the x-axis.
- `Plot[ ..., {x, 0, l}]` fills only the region to the left of `l`.
- The `Line[{{l, 0}, {l, dist@l}}]` draws the vertical segment from the x-axis to the curve.
- `Show` combines the two plots.

If you want the shaded region to be to the right of the vertical line instead, swap the plot ranges:

```wl
p1 = Plot[dist@x, {x, 0, l}, Filling -> None, PlotRange -> {{0, 10}, All}];
p2 = Plot[dist@x, {x, l, 10}, Filling -> Axis, PlotRange -> {{0, 10}, All}];
```

If you want, I can also show a cleaner one-plot version using `RegionFunction` or `FillingStyle`.

If your goal is to simplify expressions “as if” the symbols were ordinary constants, the main issue is that Mathematica treats subscripted forms like `Subscript[a,1]` as expressions, not as plain symbols. That often makes `FullSimplify` behave worse.

The usual workaround is:

1. replace subscripted variables with temporary ordinary symbols,
2. run `FullSimplify`,
3. replace the temporary symbols back.

Using your resolved thread, a reusable wrapper is:

```mathematica
fullSimplify[expr_] :=
 Module[
  {uqString, counter, definedQ, toMapper, backRules, symb, simpExpr},

  backRules = {};
  definedQ[x__] := False;
  uqString = ToString[Unique[bla]];
  counter = 0;

  Block[
   {Subscript},

   Subscript[x__] :=
    (
     If[definedQ[x],
      toMapper[x],
      definedQ[x] = True;
      symb = Symbol[uqString <> ToString[counter]];
      counter++;
      AppendTo[backRules, symb :> Subscript[x]];
      toMapper[x] = symb
     ]
    );

   simpExpr = FullSimplify[expr];
  ];

  Remove[toMapper, definedQ];
  simpExpr /. backRules
 ]
```

Then use it like:

```mathematica
fullSimplify[f1]
```

If you already have replacement rules such as `rule1`, you can apply them afterward:

```mathematica
fullSimplify[f1] /. rule1
```

Important note: this approach works best if your “subscripts” are truly just labels for variables, not objects with mathematical meaning. If possible, the cleanest long-term solution is to avoid `Subscript[...]` as variables and use plain symbols like `a1`, `a2`, `A1`, `A2`, etc.

If you want, I can also give you:
- a shorter modernized version of this wrapper,
- a version that handles `SubscriptBox`/notebook input more robustly,
- or a `ReplaceAll`-based helper for a whole set of subscripted variables.

Yes. In Mathematica/Wolfram Language, the usual way is to make the plot interactive with `DynamicModule` and update `PlotRange` from mouse actions.

For `MatrixPlot`, you can use the same idea as in the resolved thread: click to recenter, then change a magnification variable to zoom in/out. Here’s a version adapted to `MatrixPlot`:

```wl
SeedRandom[42];
data = RandomInteger[100, {100, 100}];

DynamicModule[{mag = 1, center, box, plotCenter},
  plotCenter = {.5 Dimensions[data][[1]], .5 Dimensions[data][[2]]};
  center = plotCenter;

  box[center_] := Module[
    {x = center[[1]], y = center[[2]], dx, dy, n1, n2},
    {n1, n2} = Dimensions[data];
    dx = n1/(2 mag);
    dy = n2/(2 mag);
    {{x - dx, x + dx}, {y - dy, y + dy}}
  ];

  Column[{
    ClickPane[
      Dynamic @ MatrixPlot[
        data,
        PlotRange -> box[center],
        Frame -> True,
        ImageSize -> Medium
      ],
      (center = #) &
    ],
    Dynamic @ Row[{"Magnification: ", mag, "   Center: ", center}],
    PopupMenu[
      Dynamic[mag],
      {1 -> "1x", 1.5 -> "1.5x", 2 -> "2x", 4 -> "4x"}
    ]
  }]
]
```

How it works:
- Click on the plot to move the zoom center.
- Choose a magnification value from the menu to zoom in/out.
- The visible region is controlled by `PlotRange`.

If you want mouse-wheel zoom specifically, Mathematica does not provide that automatically for `MatrixPlot` in the front end, but you can approximate it using `EventHandler` with custom mouse events. If you want, I can give you a version that supports:
- mouse drag to pan,
- mouse wheel zoom,
- click-to-center.

In short: no, “Save Graphic As…” on an Animate/Manipulate output is often not a reliable way to create an animated GIF. It may save the current frame or fail silently depending on the front end / kernel state.

A working approach is:

1. Build the animation as an `Animate`/`Manipulate` expression.
2. Export that object to a file format that supports animation.

For example, from the resolved thread:

```mathematica
prices = FinancialData["QCOM", All][[All, 2]];
S[x_] := MovingAverage[prices, x];
F[x_] := MovingAverage[prices, x];

p = Animate[
   si = Interpolation[S[s]];
   fi = Interpolation[F[f]];

   p1 = ListPlot[{prices, S[s], F[f]}, 
     PlotStyle -> {RGBColor[0.7, 0.7, 0.7], Blue, Red}, 
     Joined -> {True, True, True}, 
     PlotStyle -> {Thin, Thin, Thin}];

   p2 = Plot[{si[x], fi[x]}, {x, 1, Length[S[s]]},
     MeshFunctions -> {(si[#] - fi[#]) &}, Mesh -> {{0}},
     MeshStyle -> Directive[Red, PointSize[Large]]];

   Show[p1, p2, ImageSize -> {480, 300}],
   {{s, 150}, 150, 1000, 1},
   {{f, 79}, 79, 460, 1},
   AutorunSequencing -> All,
   AnimationDirection -> Backward
];

SetDirectory[NotebookDirectory[]];
Export["animation.swf", p]
```

That exports a playable animation file.

If you specifically need an animated GIF, the thread recommends using Vitaliy Kaurov’s ManToGif tool rather than relying on “Save Graphic As…”.

