`LogLinearPlot` stores the x-coordinates in logarithmic form internally, so when you extract the line data you get `Log[x]`, not `x`.

To recover the original x-values, apply `Exp` to the first column:

```mathematica
gr = LogLinearPlot[Sin[x], {x, 1, 10}];

data = Cases[gr[[1]], Line[data_] :> data, -4][[1]];

data = {Exp@#, #2} & @@@ data;

ListLogLinearPlot[data]
```

So for your case, do:

```mathematica
gr = LogLinearPlot[{f[r]*r, 0}, {r, 0.0001, 1000}, 
  PlotRange -> {-0.5, 0.2}, MaxRecursion -> 15];

data = Cases[gr[[1]], Line[data_] :> data, -4][[1]];

data = {Exp@#, #2} & @@@ data;

Export["~/Desktop/1.dat", SetAccuracy[data, 10], Alignment -> Center]
```

If you want the original x-values directly, another clean approach is to collect them while plotting:

```mathematica
{gr, {data}} =
  Reap[
    LogLinearPlot[f[r]*r, {r, 0.0001, 1000},
      PlotRange -> {-0.5, 0.2}, MaxRecursion -> 15,
      EvaluationMonitor :> Sow[{r, f[r]*r}]
    ]
  ];
```

Then `data` already contains the real `{r, y}` pairs, with no need to undo the log afterward.

You can do this by converting the city names to geographic coordinates, then overlaying those points on a map graphic.

A few notes first:

- `Graphics[{EdgeForm[Black], ...}] & /@ CountryData[]` won’t work the way you want, because `Graphics` expects a single graphics primitive list, not a mapped list of whole `Graphics` objects.
- For maps, it is usually easier to use `GeoGraphics` / `GeoRange` / `GeoMarker`.

Simple approach with `GeoGraphics`

If `datapoints` is a list like `{"CityName", population}` or just city names, you can do:

```wl
cities = Reverse[SortBy[DeleteCases[numberofpeople, {_, _Missing}], Last]][[1 ;; 20, 1]];

GeoGraphics[
 {
  EdgeForm[Black],
  FaceForm[None],
  GeoStyling[Entity["Country", "World"], "Polygon"],

  Red, PointSize[Medium],
  GeoMarker /@ EntityValue[Entity["City", #], "Coordinates"] & /@ cities
 },
 GeoRange -> "World"
]
```

But the cleaner version is:

```wl
cities = Reverse[SortBy[DeleteCases[numberofpeople, {_, _Missing}], Last]][[1 ;; 20, 1]];

GeoGraphics[
 {
  Entity["Country", "World"],
  Red,
  PointSize[0.015],
  GeoMarker /@ CityData /@ cities
 },
 GeoRange -> "World"
]
```

However, the exact entity form can be a little tricky depending on how your city names are stored.

Best robust method

Use city coordinates explicitly:

```wl
cities = Reverse[SortBy[DeleteCases[numberofpeople, {_, _Missing}], Last]][[1 ;; 20, 1]];

points = CityData[#, "Coordinates"] & /@ cities;

GeoGraphics[
 {
  GeoStyling[Entity["Country", "World"], "Polygon"],
  Red, PointSize[0.02],
  Point[points]
 },
 GeoRange -> "World"
]
```

If `CityData[city, "Coordinates"]` returns entities or missing values, filter them out first.

If you want to stay with `Graphics`

You’d need map coordinates in a projected coordinate system, which is more cumbersome. For world maps, `GeoGraphics` is the right tool.

Working example

```wl
numberofpeople = Map[{#, CityData[#, "Population"]} &, CityData[All]];
datapoints = Reverse[SortBy[DeleteCases[numberofpeople, {_, _Missing}], Last]][[1 ;; 20]];

cities = datapoints[[All, 1]];
coords = CityData[#, "Coordinates"] & /@ cities;

GeoGraphics[
 {
  Entity["Country", "World"],
  Red, PointSize[0.02],
  Point[coords]
 },
 GeoRange -> "World"
]
```

