You can optimize the idea by avoiding the built-in bubble sizing logic and drawing the ellipses directly from your data.

If your rows are

{xMean, xSd, yMean, ySd}

then the bubble centers are {xMean, yMean} and the ellipse radii are {xSd, ySd}. A straightforward way is to use Graphics with Ellipse:

```wl
bubbleData = {
  {1.88308*10^6, 1024.85, 149446., 6636.62},
  {1.98345*10^6, 15022.5, 126966., 7071.75},
  {1.94677*10^6, 9281.83, 131930., 7435.15},
  {1.88308*10^6, 1024.85, 127423., 8718.36}
};

Graphics[
  {
    Directive[Opacity[0.5], Blue],
    Ellipse[{#[[1]], #[[3]]}, {#[[2]], #[[4]]}] & /@ bubbleData
  },
  Axes -> True,
  PlotRange -> All
]
```

Notes:

- `Ellipse[{x, y}, {rx, ry}]` creates an axis-aligned ellipse centered at `{x, y}`.
- Here `rx = xSd` and `ry = ySd`.
- `Opacity[0.5]` makes overlaps visible.

If you want labels or tooltips, you can wrap each ellipse:

```wl
Graphics[
  Table[
    Tooltip[
      {Directive[Opacity[0.5], Blue], Ellipse[{x, y}, {rx, ry}]},
      {x, y, rx, ry}
    ],
    {{x, rx, y, ry}, bubbleData}
  ],
  Axes -> True,
  PlotRange -> All
]
```

