Yes — but for ASCII plots specifically, the usual approach is to render the graphic first and then convert it to text characters.

If your goal is a text-only “plot” in the terminal or notebook, you can make a coarse ASCII rendering by sampling the function on a grid and mapping values to characters. For example, in Mathematica/Wolfram Language:

```wl
f[x_, y_] := Sin[x y]

xmin = -2; xmax = 2;
ymin = -2; ymax = 2;

chars = " .:-=+*#%@";
n = StringLength[chars];

grid = Table[f[x, y], {y, ymax, ymin, -(ymax - ymin)/20}, {x, xmin, xmax, (xmax - xmin)/60}];

minv = Min[grid];
maxv = Max[grid];

ascii = Map[
  chars[[1 + Round[(# - minv)/(maxv - minv) (n - 1)]]]
  &, grid, {2}];

Print /@ (StringJoin /@ ascii)
```

This produces a character-based plot, with denser characters representing larger values.

If you want a better-looking result, you can also use `Rasterize` on a 2D/3D graphic and then convert the pixels to characters, but for a quick educational sketch the table-based approach is simplest.

