- a complete working Mathematica function that generates the effect automatically, or
- a version that uses only dots, not lines.

You can extract the Pareto items by:

1. sorting the list by the numeric value in descending order,
2. computing the cumulative sum,
3. taking elements until the cumulative sum reaches your cutoff, e.g. 80%.

In Mathematica/Wolfram Language:

```wl
takeParetoFromList[data_, cut_: 0.8, paretoColumn_: 2] :=
 Module[{dataSort, total, elements},
  dataSort = Reverse @ SortBy[data, #[[paretoColumn]] &];
  total = Total[data[[All, paretoColumn]]];
  elements = LengthWhile[(Accumulate @ dataSort[[All, paretoColumn]])/total, # <= cut &];
  Take[dataSort, elements]
 ]
```

Example:

```wl
list = {
 {"A&W", 15}, {"Bubbly", 19}, {"Aquabona", 42},
 {"Lanitis Extra", 91}, {"Ayataka", 80}, {"DASANI Nutriwater", 12},
 {"diet Lift/Lift light", 2}, {"Minute Maid Antiox", 74},
 {"Bimbo", 40}, {"Minute Maid Deli", 5}, {"Fire", 69}, {"Club", 14},
 {"Nada", 46}, {"Bu", 46}, {"Aybal", 29}, {"Kin", 73},
 {"Minute Maid Just 10", 31}, {"Hero", 90}, {"diet Oasis", 65}, {"Canada Dry", 30}
};

takeParetoFromList[list, 0.8, 2]
```

Result:

```wl
{
 {"Lanitis Extra", 91}, {"Hero", 90}, {"Ayataka", 80},
 {"Minute Maid Antiox", 74}, {"Kin", 73}, {"Fire", 69},
 {"diet Oasis", 65}, {"Nada", 46}, {"Bu", 46}, {"Aquabona", 42}
}
