If the condition in `If[...]` does not evaluate to `True` or `False`, then `If` does not take the first branch. Instead it returns the optional 4th argument if provided, or `Null` if not.

In your second example:

```wl
x = 1;
For[i = 1, i <= 3, i++,
  If[x + i > notDefined, x = 100];
  Print[StringJoin["i = ", ToString[i], ", x = ", ToString[x]]];
]
```

the expression `x + i > notDefined` cannot be resolved to `True` or `False` because `notDefined` has no value. So `If` leaves the body unchanged and returns `Null`.

Why no error?
- Mathematica generally does not automatically error on undeclared symbols.
- An undefined symbol is just treated as a symbolic expression.

If you want to force an error when the condition is not decidable, you can use the 4th argument of `If`, for example:

```wl
x = 1;
For[i = 1, i <= 3, i++,
  If[x + i > notDefined,
    x = 100,
    ,
    Throw["myError"]
  ];
  Print[StringJoin["i = ", ToString[i], ", x = ", ToString[x]]];
]
```

This will produce an uncaught `Throw` unless you wrap it in `Catch[...]`.

If you want, I can also show how to explicitly test whether `notDefined` has a value before comparing it.

In Mathematica, `For` is usually the wrong tool for building lists. It prints or evaluates expressions, but it does not collect results automatically.

A better approach is to use `Do` with `Sow`/`Reap`, which lets you gather only the values you want into a list.

For your case, the rough pattern would be:

```mathematica
Reap[
  Do[
    Do[
      If[Abs[Nest[f, i*StepSize1, n] - 0.51304] < 0.0001,
        Sow[i*StepSize1];
        Break[]
      ],
      {n, 100, 599}
    ],
    {i, 0, 999}
  ]
][[2, 1]]
```

This returns a list of the values you “sowed”, which you can then pass directly to `ListPlot`.

If you want to avoid the `Null`/`Indeterminate` behavior entirely, only `Sow` when the condition is met, and do nothing otherwise.

A simpler version of your original logic is:

```mathematica
