You can do this by making the ODE solution itself part of the model passed to `FindFit`, and by adding the parameter constraint with `ParameterConstraints` (or by reparameterizing if the constraint is more natural that way).

A typical pattern is:

```wl
Clear[a, b, x, y, t];

sol[t_, a_, b_] := NDSolveValue[
  {
    x'[t] == a x[t] - b y[t],
    y'[t] == b x[t] + a y[t],
    x[0] == 1, y[0] == 0
  },
  {x[t], y[t]},
  {t, 0, 10}
];
```

If your data are like `{t_i, x_i, y_i}`, you can fit both components at once by flattening them into one list and using an error vector:

```wl
data = {
  {0.1, 1.02, 0.05},
  {0.2, 1.08, 0.10},
  {0.3, 1.15, 0.16}
};

fit = FindFit[
  data,
  {
    sol[t, a, b][[1]],
    sol[t, a, b][[2]]
  },
  {{a, 1}, {b, 1}},
  t,
  ParameterConstraints -> a >= 0 && b >= 0
]
```

However, `FindFit` is mainly designed for scalar dependent data, so in practice it is often easier to use `NMinimize` on the sum of squared residuals for inverse ODE problems.

Example:

```wl
Clear[a, b];

model[t_, a_, b_] := NDSolveValue[
  {
    x'[t] == a x[t] - b y[t],
    y'[t] == b x[t] + a y[t],
    x[0] == 1, y[0] == 0
  },
  {x[t], y[t]},
  {t, 0, 10}
];

obj[a_?NumericQ, b_?NumericQ] := Module[{s},
  s = model[#, a, b] & /@ data[[All, 1]];
  Total[(Flatten[s] - Flatten[data[[All, 2 ;; 3]]])^2]
];

NMinimize[
  {obj[a, b], a >= 0 && b >= 0},
  {a, b}
]
```

If the constraint is something like `a + b == 1`, it is often better to reparameterize, for example:

```wl
b = 1 - a
```

and then fit only one free parameter.

