If your functions are defined with `Function[...]` and then reused inside `With` or `Manipulate`, Mathematica can end up showing the internal pure-function form instead of the expressions you expect, especially when using `PlotLegends -> "Expressions"`.

A cleaner way is to define the functions with explicit arguments:

```mathematica
v[x_, k_] := k*x^-k;
g[x_, k_] := (x^-k)*Sin[x^k]/(1 + x^k);
f[x_, k_] := x^k/(1 + x^k);
```

Then plot them directly:

```mathematica
Manipulate[
  Plot[
    {v[x, k], f[x, k], g[x, k], D[f[x, k] - g[x, k], x]},
    {x, 0, 5},
    PlotLegends -> {"v", "f", "g", "d(f-g)/dx"}
  ],
  {k, 1, 10}
]
```

If you want the legend to display the formulas themselves, use explicit legend formatting:

```mathematica
Manipulate[
  Plot[
    {v[x, k], f[x, k], g[x, k], D[f[x, k] - g[x, k], x]},
    {x, 0, 5},
    PlotLegends -> TraditionalForm /@ {
      v[x, k], f[x, k], g[x, k], D[f[x, k] - g[x, k], x]
    }
  ],
  {k, 1, 10}
]
```

Main point:
- `PlotLegends -> "Expressions"` works best with simple expressions.
- Pure functions and scoped variables can cause unexpected legend labels.
- Using `:=` with explicit arguments avoids the confusion.

