To obtain all minima, use the same extrema-detection idea from the previous case, but focus on the local minima positions.

If your data are noisy, first smooth/denoise, then detect minima on the cleaned signal.

Mathematica example:

```wl
temptimelist = Range[200]/10;
tempvaluelist = Sinc[#] & /@ temptimelist + RandomReal[{-1, 1}, 200]*0.02;
```

Denoise:

```wl
dwd = DiscreteWaveletTransform[tempvaluelist, SymletWavelet[7], 6];
dwd = WaveletThreshold[dwd];
est = InverseWaveletTransform[dwd];
```

Find minima positions:

```wl
findExtremaPos[list_List] := 
 Module[{signs, extremaPos, minPos, maxPos},
  signs = Sign[Differences[list]];
  signs = signs //. {a___, q_, 0, z__} -> {a, q, q, z};
  extremaPos = 1 + Accumulate@(Length /@ Split[signs]);
  If[First@signs == 1,
   minPos = extremaPos[[2 ;; -2 ;; 2]];
   maxPos = extremaPos[[1 ;; -2 ;; 2]],
   minPos = extremaPos[[1 ;; -2 ;; 2]];
   maxPos = extremaPos[[2 ;; -2 ;; 2]]
   ];
  {minPos, maxPos}
 ]
```

Apply it:

```wl
{minPos, maxPos} = findExtremaPos[est];
```

Get all minima:

```wl
minima = Transpose[{temptimelist[[minPos]], est[[minPos]]}];
```

If you want a built-in alternative, `FindPeaks` can help for maxima; minima can be found by applying it to `-est`:

```wl
minima = FindPeaks[-est]
```

If your data are not noisy, the simple sign-change method is enough. If they are noisy, smoothing is usually necessary to avoid detecting many spurious minima.

