If you have many functions and want the region where one of them is the largest, `RegionPlot` is the right tool.

For example, if `f1` should be larger than all the others:

```mathematica
RegionPlot[
  And @@ (f1[x, y] >= # & /@ {f2[x, y], f3[x, y], f4[x, y]}),
  {x, xmin, xmax}, {y, ymin, ymax}
]
```

More generally, if you want to plot the region where the maximum occurs for each function, you can do:

```mathematica
funcs = {f1[x, y], f2[x, y], f3[x, y], f4[x, y]};

RegionPlot[
  And @@ (funcs[[1]] >= Rest[funcs]),
  {x, xmin, xmax}, {y, ymin, ymax}
]
```

If you want the region where any one function is maximal, you usually make one `RegionPlot` per function and combine them, for example:

```mathematica
RegionPlot[
  f1[x, y] >= f2[x, y] && f1[x, y] >= f3[x, y] && f1[x, y] >= f4[x, y],
  {x, xmin, xmax}, {y, ymin, ymax}
]
```

If you have a lot of functions and want this automated:

```mathematica
funcs = {f1, f2, f3, f4};

regions = Table[
  RegionPlot[
    And @@ Table[
      funcs[[i]][x, y] >= funcs[[j]][x, y],
      {j, DeleteCases[Range[Length[funcs]], i]}
    ],
    {x, xmin, xmax}, {y, ymin, ymax},
    PlotLabel -> ToString[funcs[[i]]]
  ],
  {i, Length[funcs]}
];
```

Then `regions` contains one plot for each function’s “winning” region.

If you mean something slightly different by “many functions” — for example, plotting the region where the maximum of all functions exceeds a threshold — tell me the exact condition and I can write the Mathematica code for that.

If you want a **region plot of which function is largest** at each point, the usual way is to compare them pairwise and combine the inequalities.

For functions `f1[x]`, `f2[x]`, `f3[x]`, etc., the region where `f1` is the largest is:

```mathematica
RegionPlot[
  f1[x, y] >= f2[x, y] && f1[x, y] >= f3[x, y] && f1[x, y] >= f4[x, y],
  {x, xmin, xmax}, {y, ymin, ymax}
]
```

If you want to label the regions for the largest among several functions, a practical approach is to use `Which` or `Max` with `RegionPlot`-style logic.

Example for three functions:

```mathematica
RegionPlot[
  Evaluate[
    {
      f1[x, y] >= f2[x, y] && f1[x, y] >= f3[x, y],
      f2[x, y] >= f1[x, y] && f2[x, y] >= f3[x, y],
      f3[x, y] >= f1[x, y] && f3[x, y] >= f2[x, y]
    }
  ],
  {x, xmin, xmax}, {y, ymin, ymax},
  PlotLegends -> {"f1 largest", "f2 largest", "f3 largest"}
]
```

If you have a list of functions, say:

```mathematica
fs = {f1, f2, f3, f4};
```

then you can build the conditions automatically:

```mathematica
RegionPlot[
  Evaluate@Table[
    And @@ Table[
      fs[[i]][x, y] >= fs[[j]][x, y],
      {j, Length[fs]}, {i, Length[fs]}
    ],
    {i, Length[fs]}
  ],
  {x, xmin, xmax}, {y, ymin, ymax}
]
```

A cleaner version is to use `Max` and `Boole`/`Piecewise` if you want coloring by winner. For example, to color by which function attains the maximum, use `DensityPlot` with an index function rather than `RegionPlot`.

