You can sort one list according to the order defined by another by building a lookup table from the reference list, then using that to sort the target list.

In Wolfram Language, a common pattern is:

```wl
reference = {"b", "c", "a"};
items = {"a", "b", "c", "a", "b"};

pos = AssociationThread[reference -> Range[Length[reference]]];
SortBy[items, pos[#] &]
```

This returns:

```wl
{"b", "b", "c", "a", "a"}
```

If your data are rows and you want to reorder each row based on a header/order list, you can do:

```wl
order = {"T1K", "Vg2V", "Ch3"};
rows = {
  {"Ch3", "T1K", "Vg2V"},
  {"Vg2V", "Ch3", "T1K"}
};

SortBy[#, pos[#] &] & /@ rows
```

If the list to sort contains elements not in the reference list, you can give them a default priority, for example:

```wl
SortBy[items, Lookup[pos, #, Infinity] &]
```

