What’s happening is that the code inside the Button is being evaluated in the usual notebook/front-end way, so it can appear to “stop” or get interrupted after a few seconds if:

- the button action returns control to the front end,
- there’s a front-end timing/interaction issue,
- or the code is being evaluated in a way that isn’t meant for long-running computations.

If your goal is to run something and then notify you when it finishes, the key point from the resolved case is:

- you can’t use `EvaluationCompletionAction` to decide based on how long the evaluation took,
- because it only fires after completion and does not provide elapsed time to test.

A better pattern is to time the computation yourself, for example:

```wl
Button["Run",
  Module[{start, result},
    start = AbsoluteTime[];
    result = yourExpression;
    If[AbsoluteTime[] - start > 30, Beep[]];
    result
  ]
]
```

If the button action itself seems to “stop after some seconds,” a more robust workaround is to move the long computation out of the immediate button handler, for example:

```wl
Button["Run",
  CreateScheduledTask[
    Module[{start, result},
      start = AbsoluteTime[];
      result = yourExpression;
      If[AbsoluteTime[] - start > 30, Beep[]];
      result
    ],
    {}
  ] // StartScheduledTask
]
```

Or, if you just want the button to launch the computation without front-end interference:

```wl
Button["Run", FrontEndExecute@FrontEnd`CellPrint@Cell[BoxData@ToBoxes@yourExpression, "Input"]]
```

More simply, if the issue is that the button itself is being used for a long-running evaluation, I’d recommend not putting the full computation directly inside the button. Use the button only to trigger a separate evaluation, then time/notify from there.

