If the plots are just based on the same kind of data, you can usually generate them in a loop or with `Map`/`Table` rather than writing each case separately. The idea is:

1. split the data by category,
2. make one plot for each category,
3. arrange the results in a grid.

For example:

```mathematica
groups = GroupBy[t, First];
plots = ListLinePlot[#[[All, 2 ;; 3]]] & /@ Values[groups];

GraphicsGrid[Partition[plots, 2]]
```

If you want all plots to be directly comparable, give them the same `PlotRange`:

```mathematica
range = {
   {Min@Flatten[t[[All, 2]]], Max@Flatten[t[[All, 2]]]},
   {Min@Flatten[t[[All, 3]]], Max@Flatten[t[[All, 3]]]}
};

plots = ListLinePlot[#[[All, 2 ;; 3]], PlotRange -> range] & /@ Values[groups];
GraphicsGrid[Partition[plots, 2]]
```

So the short answer is: no, you do not have to code each plot separately unless each one is truly different. If they follow the same pattern, generate them programmatically.

