```wl
{Cos[a], Sin[a]}
```

and the tangent direction is:

```wl
{-Sin[a], Cos[a]}
```

If you want, I can adapt this to:
- an arbitrary curve `x[t], y[t]`
- a symbolic curve
- a 3D curve

Use the derivative of the curve to get the tangent direction, then place that tangent at the moving point.

For a parametric curve

x[t], y[t]

the tangent vector at t = a is

{x'[a], y'[a]}

If you want a tangent line segment of fixed length, normalize that vector and scale it:

L Normalize[{x'[a], y'[a]}]

Then draw it starting at the point on the curve:

Arrow[{{x[a], y[a]}, {x[a], y[a]} + L Normalize[{x'[a], y'[a]}]}]

Example for a circle:

x[t_] := Cos[t];
y[t_] := Sin[t];

Manipulate[
 Show[
  ParametricPlot[{x[t], y[t]}, {t, 0, 2 Pi}, PlotRange -> 2],
  Graphics[
   {
    Red,
    Arrow[{{x[a], y[a]}, {x[a], y[a]} + Normalize[{-Sin[a], Cos[a]}]}]
   }
  ]
 ],
 {a, 0, 2 Pi}
]

This makes the tangent move along the curve as a changes.

