If the diagram you want is a phase-space plot for a time-dependent system, Mathematica won’t give a true 2D autonomous phase portrait directly, because the system is nonautonomous. But you can still draw the trajectories in the $(x, x')$ plane very nicely.

The simplest approach is to use EquationTrekker:

```mathematica
<< EquationTrekker`

EquationTrekker[
  x''[t] - (1 - x[t]^2) x'[t] + x[t] == 0.5 Cos[1.1 t],
  x[t], {t, 0, 10}
]
```

This opens an interactive phase-space window. You can click or right-click in the plot to generate trajectories from chosen initial conditions.

If your diagram depends on parameters, use:

```mathematica
EquationTrekker[
  x''[t] - (1 - x[t]^2) x'[t] + x[t] == a Cos[\[Omega] t],
  x[t], {t, 0, 10},
  TrekParameters -> {a -> 0.5, \[Omega] -> 1.1}
]
```

If you want a more manual approach, you can also solve the system numerically and plot $x(t)$ versus $x'(t)$:

```mathematica
sol = NDSolve[
  {
    x''[t] - (1 - x[t]^2) x'[t] + x[t] == 0.5 Cos[1.1 t],
    x[0] == 1, x'[0] == 0
  },
  x, {t, 0, 50}
];

ParametricPlot[
  Evaluate[{x[t], x'[t]} /. sol],
  {t, 0, 50},
  AxesLabel -> {"x", "x'"}
]
```

