Yes — for pre-sorted lists, `Complement` works well for removing items already present in earlier lists.

If the lists are sorted and you want to preserve that ordering, you can do:

```mathematica
Complement[list2, list1]
Complement[list3, Union[list1, list2]]
```

For your example:

```mathematica
list1 = {2, 5, 10, 11, 12, 17, 18};
list2 = {5, 8, 11, 16, 19, 21};
list3 = {5, 8, 9, 11, 16, 19, 21, 23};

{list1, Complement[list2, list1], Complement[list3, Union[list1, list2]]}
```

gives:

```mathematica
{
 {2, 5, 10, 11, 12, 17, 18},
 {8, 16, 19, 21},
 {9, 23}
}
```

If you have a whole list of lists, you can do this incrementally:

```mathematica
lists = {list1, list2, list3};

FoldList[Complement, First[lists], Rest[lists]]
```

Or, more generally, if you want each list minus all previous ones:

```mathematica
accumulate[seen_, lst_] := Complement[lst, seen]

seen = {};
result = Table[
  new = Complement[lists[[i]], seen];
  seen = Union[seen, lists[[i]]];
  new,
  {i, Length[lists]}
];
```

If you want, I can give a one-line version that handles any number of pre-sorted lists.

If your lists are already sorted, you can use `Complement` directly, and it will preserve the sorted structure of the result.

Example:

```mathematica
list1 = {2, 5, 10, 11, 12, 17, 18};
list2 = {5, 8, 11, 16, 19, 21};
list3 = {5, 8, 9, 11, 16, 19, 21, 23};

Complement[list2, list1]
Complement[list3, Union[list1, list2]]
```

Result:

```mathematica
{8, 16, 19, 21}
{9, 23}
```

If you want to remove elements from each list that already appeared in earlier lists, use `FoldList`:

```mathematica
lists = {list1, list2, list3};
cleaned = FoldList[Complement[#2, Union @@ #1] &, First[lists], Rest[lists]];
```

This gives the successive cleaned lists.

