If you mean in Wolfram Language: you can revolve a curve/surface around an arbitrary line by first moving and rotating the coordinate system so that the line becomes one of the coordinate axes, doing the revolution, and then transforming back.

A common pattern is:

1. Translate so the line passes through the origin.
2. Rotate so the line aligns with the z-axis.
3. Revolve around the z-axis.
4. Undo the rotation and translation.

For example, if your axis is the line through point p0 in direction v, you can use `RotationTransform` and `TranslationTransform`.

Example skeleton:

```wl
p0 = {1, 2, 3};          (* a point on the axis *)
v  = {1, 1, 0};          (* direction of the axis *)

axisToZ = RotationTransform[{{v[[1]], v[[2]], v[[3]]}, {0, 0, 1}}];

toStandard = Composition[
  axisToZ,
  TranslationTransform[-p0]
];

fromStandard = InverseFunction[toStandard];
```

Then define your generating curve/surface in the transformed coordinates, revolve it around the z-axis, and map it back with `GeometricTransformation`.

If you already have a 2D profile curve and want the 3D surface, `RevolutionPlot3D` is the direct tool, but it assumes the axis is one of the coordinate axes. For an arbitrary axis, the usual workaround is to transform the curve first.

For example, if you want to revolve a curve around the line through `{1,2,3}` in direction `{1,1,0}`:

```wl
p0 = {1, 2, 3};
v = Normalize[{1, 1, 0}];

rot = RotationTransform[{{v[[1]], v[[2]], v[[3]]}, {0, 0, 1}}];
trans = TranslationTransform[-p0];

f = Composition[rot, trans];
finv = InverseFunction[f];

curve = ParametricPlot3D[
  finv[{r[t] Cos[u], r[t] Sin[u], z[t]}],
  {t, tmin, tmax}, {u, 0, 2 Pi}
];
```

If you want, I can give you a concrete `RevolutionPlot3D` example for a specific line and generating curve.

If you want to manipulate a circular part of an image in Wolfram Language, the usual approach is to work with image masks or overlay graphics on top of the image.

A few common ways:

1. Crop or isolate a circular region
Use a mask based on distance from a center point:

```wl
img = Import["image.jpg"];
mask = Binarize @ DistanceTransform[ColorNegate @ ImageCrop[ImageDisk[]]];
```

But more directly, you can create a circular mask:

```wl
dims = ImageDimensions[img];
center = dims/2;
radius = Min[dims]/3;

mask = ImageGraphics[
  {White, Disk[center, radius]},
  ImageSize -> dims
];

circularPart = ImageMultiply[img, mask];
```

2. Use RegionFunction / graphics-based editing
If you are drawing or reconstructing the image, you can use `Graphics` primitives like `Circle`, `Disk`, `Line`, and `Point` to represent and edit circular parts.

Example:

```wl
Graphics[
 {
  LightGray, Disk[{0, 0}, 1],
  Red, Circle[{0, 0}, 1],
  Blue, PointSize[0.03], Point[{1/Sqrt[2], 1/Sqrt[2]}]
 },
 Axes -> True
]
```

3. Overlay or modify only inside a circle
You can apply a transformation only within a circular area by combining a mask with `ImageCompose`:

```wl
img2 = ImageAdjust[img];
mask = Graphics[
  {White, Disk[ImageDimensions[img]/2, 100]},
  PlotRange -> {{0, ImageDimensions[img][[1]]}, {0, ImageDimensions[img][[2]]}},
  ImageSize -> ImageDimensions[img]
];

result = ImageCompose[img, img2, mask];
```

If your goal is more like the resolved case you referenced: a circular part is typically represented with `Circle` or `Disk` in `Graphics`, and anything inside or around it is manipulated by changing the coordinates of lines, points, or other primitives.

For example, to scale a circular construction, multiply all coordinates by the desired radius and keep the circle centered:

```wl
Graphics[
 {
  Red, Line[{{0, 0}, {3/Sqrt[2], 3/Sqrt[2]}}],
  Blue, Circle[{0, 0}, 3]
 },
 Axes -> True
]
```

