For a snowflake-like shape, you can use random radii around a circle:

```wl
randomSnowflake[n_Integer : 12] := Module[{angles, radii, pts},
  angles = Subdivide[0, 2 Pi, n - 1][[;; -2]];
  radii = 1 + 0.35 RandomReal[{-1, 1}, n];
  pts = Transpose[{radii Cos[angles], radii Sin[angles]}];
  Polygon[pts]
]

Graphics[{EdgeForm[Black], LightBlue, randomSnowflake[16]}]
```

This gives a radial, irregular shape that looks snowflake-like.

If you want it to be more “snowflake” and less just a wavy polygon, you can add symmetry by repeating one random arm around the center:

```wl
randomSnowflake[k_Integer : 6] := Module[{base, arm},
  arm = 1 + 0.4 RandomReal[{-1, 1}, 2 k];
  base = Table[
    RotationTransform[2 Pi i/k][
      Polygon[Transpose[{arm Cos[Subdivide[0, Pi, 2 k - 1]], 
                         arm Sin[Subdivide[0, Pi, 2 k - 1]]}]]
    ],
    {i, 0, k - 1}
  ];
  Graphics[{LightBlue, EdgeForm[Black], GeometricTransformation[Polygon[], Identity]}]
]
```

A more practical version is to generate one “arm” and rotate it:

```wl
