Yes — for nested optimization in Mathematica, the main idea is to avoid recomputing the `j`-dependent quantities inside the innermost loop.

A good pattern is:

- compute `p`, `q`, `r`, `mat` once per `j`
- then reuse them for all `k`

For example:

```mathematica
Table[
  Table[
    Module[{p, q, r, mat},
      p = test[[i, j, 1]];
      q = test[[i, j, 2]];
      r = Cross[p, q];
      mat = Orthogonalize[{p, q, r}];
      Table[
        mat.SeparationVector[p, test[[i, k, 1]], 2],
        {k, 5}
      ]
    ],
    {j, 5}
  ],
  {i, 10}
]
```

If you want the same flattened structure as your original `res`, use:

```mathematica
Flatten[
  Table[
    Table[
      Module[{p, q, r, mat},
        p = test[[i, j, 1]];
        q = test[[i, j, 2]];
        r = Cross[p, q];
        mat = Orthogonalize[{p, q, r}];
        Table[
          mat.SeparationVector[p, test[[i, k, 1]], 2],
          {k, 5}
        ]
      ],
      {j, 5}
    ],
    {i, 10}
  ],
  2
]
```

A couple of performance notes:

- Prefer `Table` over repeated `AppendTo`
- Use `Module` or `With` to localize and reuse intermediate values
- If `mat` does not depend on `k`, make sure it is not inside the `k` loop

