Yes — you can use `Manipulate` with `Epilog` (or `Graphics`) to draw the point on the curve and a tangent vector at that point.

For a parametric curve `{x[t], y[t]}`, the tangent vector is given by the derivative:

```mathematica
{x'[t], y'[t]}
```

A simple example:

```mathematica
x[t_] := t;
y[t_] := 2 t Sin[t];

Manipulate[
 Graphics[
  {
   Blue, Thick, ParametricPlot[{x[s], y[s]}, {s, 0, 10},
     PlotStyle -> Blue, PlotRange -> All][[1]],

   Red, PointSize[Large], Point[{x[a], y[a]}],

   Black, Arrow[{{x[a], y[a]},
     {x[a], y[a]} + 0.5 Normalize[{x'[a], y'[a]}]}}]
  },
  PlotRange -> {{0, 10}, All}
 ],
 {a, 0, 10}
]
```

If your curve is just `y = f(x)`, then the tangent vector at `x = a` is:

```mathematica
{1, f'[a]}
```

and you can display it like this:

```mathematica
f[x_] := 2 x Sin[x];

Manipulate[
 Plot[f[x], {x, 0, 10},
  PlotRange -> All,
  Epilog -> {
    Red, PointSize[Large], Point[{a, f[a]}],
    Black, Arrow[{{a, f[a]}, {a, f[a]} + 0.8 Normalize[{1, f'[a]}]}]
  }],
 {a, 0, 10}
]
```

This shows:
- the point on the curve in red
- the tangent vector as a black arrow

If you want, I can also show how to draw the tangent line together with the tangent vector in the same `Manipulate`.

You can do it by plotting the curve, then adding:

1. a point marker at the selected parameter value, and  
2. a short line segment in the direction of the derivative there.

For a parameterized curve `r[t_]`, the tangent vector is `r'[t]`.

Example:

```wl
r[t_] := {Cos[t], Sin[t], t};
rp[t_] := D[r[s], s] /. s -> t;

t0 = 2 Pi;
len = 0.5;

Show[
  ParametricPlot3D[r[t], {t, 0, 6 Pi}],
  Graphics3D[{
    Red, PointSize[Large], Point[r[t0]],
    Blue, Thick, Line[{r[t0], r[t0] + len Normalize[rp[t0]]}]
  }]
]
```

If you want several points and tangent vectors:

```wl
Show[
  ParametricPlot3D[r[t], {t, 0, 6 Pi}],
  Graphics3D[
    Table[
      {
        Red, PointSize[Medium], Point[r[t]],
        Blue, Thick, Line[{r[t], r[t] + 0.4 Normalize[rp[t]]}]
      },
      {t, 0, 6 Pi, Pi/4}
    ]
  ]
]
```

If you want the tangent vector shown with its actual length instead of normalized, use:

```wl
Line[{r[t0], r[t0] + 0.2 rp[t0]}]
```

