This is the same issue as in the previous case: `NDSolve` needs a numeric parameter before it can solve, so the solution should be computed as a function of the parameter first.

Use `ParametricNDSolve` (or `NDSolveValue`) and then insert the parameter inside `Manipulate`.

Example:

```wl
c = 0.05;
f = 0.7;

sol = ParametricNDSolve[
   {
    x''[t] + c x'[t] + Sin[x[t]] + f Cos[w t] == 0,
    x[0] == 1,
    x'[0] == 0
   },
   x,
   {t, 0, 10},
   {w}
];

Manipulate[
 With[{x1 = x[w] /. sol},
  Plot[x1[t], {t, 0, 10},
   PlotRange -> All,
   AxesLabel -> {"t", "x"}]
 ],
 {w, 0.1, 1.5, 0.1}
]
```

Why this fixes it:
- `w` is treated as a parameter, not a variable during solving.
- The ODE is solved once in parametric form.
- `Manipulate` only evaluates the already-defined solution for each `w`.

If you want, I can also show the cleaner `NDSolveValue` version.

For two `ListContourPlot`s, use the same `PlotRange` and the same `AspectRatio` so they share the same coordinate scale.

Example:

```mathematica
pr = {{xmin, xmax}, {ymin, ymax}};

p1 = ListContourPlot[data1, PlotRange -> pr, AspectRatio -> 1];
p2 = ListContourPlot[data2, PlotRange -> pr, AspectRatio -> 1];
```

If you want to compare them side by side, also keep the same image size:

```mathematica
ListContourPlot[data1, PlotRange -> pr, AspectRatio -> 1, ImageSize -> 300]
ListContourPlot[data2, PlotRange -> pr, AspectRatio -> 1, ImageSize -> 300]
```

If the data ranges are not known in advance, compute a common range from both datasets first:

```mathematica
all = Join[Flatten[data1, 1], Flatten[data2, 1]];
xmin = Min[all[[All, 1]]];
xmax = Max[all[[All, 1]]];
ymin = Min[all[[All, 2]]];
ymax = Max[all[[All, 2]]];

pr = {{xmin, xmax}, {ymin, ymax}};
```

Then use `pr` for both plots.

If you are instead asking for the contour levels themselves to match, that is separate: set the same `Contours` and `ColorFunctionScaling` options on both plots.

If you mean “write only the nth line to a file,” the simplest Wolfram Language pattern is to open the file and write just that line when you reach it.

Example: write only line n

```wl
n = 5;
stream = OpenWrite["output.txt"];

Do[
  If[i == n,
    WriteString[stream, "This is line " <> ToString[i] <> "\n"]
  ],
  {i, 1, 10}
];

Close[stream];
```

If you already have the line content and just want to write one specific line:

```wl
n = 5;
lines = Table["Line " <> ToString[i], {i, 1, 10}];

Export["output.txt", lines[[n]], "Text"]
```

If you want to write the nth line of a larger list of strings to an existing file by appending:

```wl
n = 5;
lines = Table["Line " <> ToString[i], {i, 1, 10}];

stream = OpenAppend["output.txt"];
WriteString[stream, lines[[n]] <> "\n"];
Close[stream];
```

If by “nth line” you mean “write every nth line only” from a list, use `Mod`:

```wl
lines = Table["Line " <> ToString[i], {i, 1, 10}];

stream = OpenWrite["output.txt"];
Do[
  If[Mod[i, 3] == 0,
    WriteString[stream, lines[[i]] <> "\n"]
  ],
  {i, Length[lines]}
];
Close[stream];
```

If you want, I can also show how to do this from a file read in line-by-line, or how to overwrite just the nth line in an existing file.

Yes — you can export the animation as a GIF and then use it in PowerPoint.

Typical workflow in Mathematica:

1. Generate the animation frames
2. Export them as a `.gif`
3. Insert the GIF into PowerPoint

Example:

```mathematica
frames = Table[
   Plot[
    Sin[x],
    {x, 0, 10},
    PlotRange -> {{0, 10}, {r, r + 5}}
   ],
   {r, 0, 99}
];

Export["C:\\temp\\animation.gif", frames]
```

If your frames are created in a `Do` loop, collect them in a list first:

```mathematica
frames = {};
Do[
  AppendTo[
    frames,
    Plot[
      Sin[x],
      {x, 0, 10},
      PlotRange -> {{0, 10}, {r, r + 5}}
    ]
  ],
  {r, 0, 99}
];

Export["C:\\temp\\animation.gif", frames]
```

Then in PowerPoint:
- use Insert > Pictures
- select the `.gif`
- note that animated GIF playback can depend on the PowerPoint version/settings

If the GIF does not animate properly in PowerPoint, exporting a video such as `.mp4` is often a better option.

